WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.023
- Good evening.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:12.830
Welcome, welcome, welcome.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:15.040
For some of you, can you
just raise your hand?

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:19.083
Is this your first event
as part of this weekend?

00:00:24.367 --> 00:00:27.250
Okay, welcome, welcome, welcome.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:29.930
Part of my responsibility
for welcoming you

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:34.440
and my pleasure in welcoming
you to this part of the event

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:39.440
is to let you understand
how this all evolved.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:44.630
Please know that the people
on the event committee,

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:47.543
led so ably by Eve Hart Rice,

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.570
so ably by Eve Hart Rice,

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:57.570
never felt that this was the culmination

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:00.240
of acknowledging the first women.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:01.473
This is the launch of
acknowledging first woman.

00:01:01.473 --> 00:01:02.306
(applause)

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:11.150
This evening, this weekend,
started almost four years ago

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:16.043
at the lunch table of Marta Moret.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:18.538
She brought together, for whatever reason,

00:01:18.538 --> 00:01:22.340
a number of women.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:23.716
It was a small group,
but included was Eve Rice

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:28.716
from the class of '73, Connie
Royster from the class of '72.

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:34.650
(applause)

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:37.313
I was there from the class of '71.

00:01:38.314 --> 00:01:39.220
(applause)

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:44.220
Linda Lorimer was there
as being a pivotal person

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:46.607
involved and interested in
what's happening with women

00:01:46.607 --> 00:01:48.853
and what's happening
across the university.

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:53.090
And a number of other people
kind of came in and out

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:53.923
with some of those
meetings, but it started

00:01:53.923 --> 00:01:54.756
almost four years ago as
we were talking about women

00:01:54.756 --> 00:01:59.690
at Yale, what's happening now,
what happened in the past,

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:05.960
how do we acknowledge our past?

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:07.194
How does that get infused, and
transferred, and documented,

00:02:07.194 --> 00:02:12.194
and encouraged among the
women who are here now?

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:15.970
How have we gone out into the world?

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:17.293
How did we get here?

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:21.150
And in that process, as
we talked about women

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:26.150
across Yale, and realized
upcoming was the 150th anniversary

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:27.869
of women at the university
because the first ones came in

00:02:27.869 --> 00:02:32.869
1869, and those of us who came
to the college came in 1969,

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:39.668
we wanted to do things that would really

00:02:39.668 --> 00:02:44.668
put in perspective what did we face.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:47.900
We know that there were
women who came before us

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.200
who had it harder than we did.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:51.509
What happened to those women
who came when there was only

00:02:51.509 --> 00:02:55.580
one woman here at the law school?

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:58.670
Or only two people at the graduate school?

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:01.360
Or only maybe one person
at the medical school?

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:03.850
We know they had a difficult time.

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:05.186
And so, we are not here to
diminish anything that they went

00:03:05.186 --> 00:03:10.186
through, or however
they handled themselves,

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:12.790
or what the university was like then,

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:16.340
but we know that even women
who came to the college,

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:21.340
maybe in 1967, or even 1980,
may have had a difficult time.

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:23.970
It's not to diminish that.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:25.014
But one of the things
we felt truly was that

00:03:25.014 --> 00:03:30.014
the women who came when we
did had a unique experience.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:33.101
It took having a critical
mass coming at the same time

00:03:33.101 --> 00:03:33.934
specifically to the college
that changed the life

00:03:33.934 --> 00:03:38.934
of every woman at this university.

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:47.220
It meant a push for more faculty,

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:49.870
it meant a push for more administrators,

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:52.670
it meant a push for all kinds of things.

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:57.190
And in this conversation, we
acknowledge that there were men

00:03:57.190 --> 00:03:58.527
in our classes, and who preceded us

00:03:58.527 --> 00:04:01.360
who helped to make this happen.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:04.320
We do not diminish that at all.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.750
And we knew that there
were men who came with us,

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:10.180
who really are our staunch allies.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:13.610
But the key thing that we
kept saying to ourselves is,

00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:17.720
many of us never knew each
other when we were here.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:22.630
We were spread out across
the 12 residential colleges.

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:25.360
And even the women who
came and were at Vanderbilt

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:29.080
were not necessarily
encouraged to meet together,

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:31.160
and it's not as though
we wanted to be isolated

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:34.300
from anybody in this room who was really

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:36.130
one of our staunch allies.

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:39.870
But we felt that this
was our pivotal chance

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:44.840
to encourage, invite, acknowledge,

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:49.840
and see all around us the
women we had never met before,

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:55.190
or who only saw for a few
times on campus 50 years ago,

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:59.200
but had never kept in touch with.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:04.200
And so that's why the focus
was to have the core women

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:09.430
who came in 1969,
whether we stayed or not,

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:11.980
the pivotal thing was to keep saying,

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:14.970
what would bring us together,
what would acknowledge,

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:17.450
what would make people
feel safe, protected,

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:20.690
feel like they want to tell their stories.

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:22.380
We wanted to document it in the way

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:24.810
that might be best for everybody.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:27.380
And we know that some people
have come back to reunions,

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:29.220
and we're glad of that.

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:30.637
And that we have heard there are people

00:05:30.637 --> 00:05:33.980
who've never stepped foot in Yale again.

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:38.210
So, we would rather err on the
side of repeating something

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:41.580
that maybe some people
saw before or heard before

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:43.940
so that the people who never came back

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:46.700
felt that they had a unifying experience

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:49.060
and that they were
understanding and seeing

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:53.690
what has changed at Yale, how
you made an impact at Yale,

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:56.260
what your legacy is here at Yale.

00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:59.840
So, having said those things,
I try to at least acknowledge

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:03.310
to those of you who may have felt left out

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:08.310
why we did what we did, how
we came to be here right now,

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:09.979
and to welcome you to
understanding why we admire you,

00:06:09.979 --> 00:06:14.979
we respect you, but we felt
that we needed to have time

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:22.070
to meet with one another.

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:23.167
So that, in some of the sessions
that are really closed to

00:06:23.167 --> 00:06:28.167
anybody other than the people
from 1969, please understand,

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:32.840
it's that we felt that there
needed to be some times

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:36.390
when we looked to the right,
when we looked to the left,

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:38.710
maybe we didn't know those women then,

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:41.010
but they were part of the sisterhood

00:06:41.010 --> 00:06:45.130
that was here at a critical
time in Yale's history.

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:47.813
And that is the emphasis of this weekend.

00:06:48.685 --> 00:06:49.680
(applause)

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:50.513
That's why.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:55.160
So, welcome, welcome, welcome,

00:06:55.160 --> 00:07:00.160
and it's my pleasure to salute all of us

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:01.618
by having a toast and asking

00:07:01.618 --> 00:07:05.290
the president of Yale to come forward.

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:09.853
Now, most of you have
heard his stellar resume,

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:14.550
and there's actually a glass
up here for you, Peter.

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:16.950
Most of you have heard her stellar resume,

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:21.720
and you know the long version
of all of his accolades.

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:22.873
He is a man who has been
here who came to do his PhD

00:07:22.873 --> 00:07:27.873
and he met his wife at that point in time.

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:31.840
They have been here on
the university campus

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:36.840
where he is now the Chris
Argyris Professor of Psychology.

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:39.880
He has played a role as
being not only a professor,

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:41.191
but chair of his department,

00:07:41.191 --> 00:07:43.210
and the dean of the graduate school,

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:46.433
the dean of the college, the provost.

00:07:48.353 --> 00:07:51.050
And he may be the 23rd president of Yale,

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:53.640
but my favorite introduction to him

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:58.640
is he is the man who is smart
enough, confident enough,

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:03.060
and lucky enough to be
married to Marta Moret,

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:03.893
Peter Salovey.

00:08:03.893 --> 00:08:04.726
(applause)

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:06.970
- Vera, that was great.

00:08:08.950 --> 00:08:10.100
- And here's your wine.

00:08:12.488 --> 00:08:13.321
- All right, thank you.

00:08:13.321 --> 00:08:14.154
Vera, thank you.

00:08:14.154 --> 00:08:16.040
That was an amazing
introduction, I have to say.

00:08:16.040 --> 00:08:19.893
One that I loved, and Marta
loved even more, I think.

00:08:21.290 --> 00:08:25.340
Well, look at yourselves, this
is an amazing, amazing group.

00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:26.675
I can't tell you how thrilling
it is for Marta and for me

00:08:26.675 --> 00:08:31.675
to be here with you, and to
welcome you back to campus.

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:35.692
It is a pleasure, it's an honor,

00:08:35.692 --> 00:08:40.692
it's humbling, but it's also fantastic.

00:08:43.635 --> 00:08:45.580
And thank you, thank you for coming back.

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:47.280
You know, Marta and I
have another privilege,

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:50.160
which is people thanked us
when we were greeting people

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:52.080
as they came in the door, you all,

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:55.890
and people saying, "Thank you
for putting this on for us."

00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:59.563
And we didn't put it on for you.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:05.710
I just am lucky enough to
work with a group of people

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:10.710
who are willing to labor
hard in putting wonderful,

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:14.810
wonderful tributes, wonderful events,

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:16.148
wonderful commemorations together

00:09:16.148 --> 00:09:18.970
on behalf of this university.

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:22.578
And the truth is, Marta got to say that,

00:09:22.578 --> 00:09:24.947
at that night that Vera was talking about,

00:09:24.947 --> 00:09:26.860
"Let's have a celebration."

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:30.650
And the next thing you know,
volunteers and Yale staff

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:32.760
are working together to make it happen.

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:34.114
And I just get to say, "Thank
you" to all those people

00:09:34.114 --> 00:09:39.114
who pulled this weekend together.

00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:48.460
To Eve, to Linda, and to
all the committee people,

00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:52.600
too many to mention by name,
thank you for what you did.

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:54.170
What people are telling
me is that they're having

00:09:54.170 --> 00:09:56.700
the time of their life,
they're delighted to be back,

00:09:56.700 --> 00:10:01.400
and that makes us all so so grateful.

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:03.688
So, what a milestone.

00:10:03.688 --> 00:10:07.870
50 years since coeducation at Yale.

00:10:07.870 --> 00:10:09.290
And in our panel after dinner,

00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:12.923
we'll hear more about how that happened.

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:18.133
But you all changed campus forever.

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:22.802
You all, that is right, give
yourself a round of applause.

00:10:22.802 --> 00:10:23.635
(applause)

00:10:23.635 --> 00:10:25.640
I know you've been doing
that all day, but ...

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:32.640
Yale is an immeasurably
better place in 2019,

00:10:35.210 --> 00:10:37.913
than it was, certainly, in 1701,

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:43.840
because of what you did in 1969.

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:46.083
And we are thankful for that.

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:51.150
We are pleased to welcome so
many of you back to campus,

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:52.308
and we are especially gratified

00:10:52.308 --> 00:10:55.690
to hear about your experiences,

00:10:55.690 --> 00:10:59.270
whether we're speaking
one-on-one or as part of a panel.

00:10:59.270 --> 00:11:02.870
Thank you for sharing all of that with us.

00:11:02.870 --> 00:11:04.169
So as we commemorate 50 years,
we salute the classes of

00:11:04.169 --> 00:11:05.002
1970, 1971, 1972, 1973, there was no 1970,

00:11:05.002 --> 00:11:10.002
one, two, and three, I got a
little ahead of myself there.

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:24.270
We wish there had been a
1970, but '71, '72, and '73.

00:11:25.100 --> 00:11:26.294
Your courage, your determination
inspire us and inspire

00:11:26.294 --> 00:11:31.294
every member of the Yale community.

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:39.057
So let's raise a glass to the
trailblazers of coeducation

00:11:44.090 --> 00:11:48.580
in Yale College to all
the remarkable women

00:11:48.580 --> 00:11:52.340
who have made Yale what it is today.

00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:53.203
Cheers.

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:56.180
(applause)

00:11:56.180 --> 00:11:59.970
- It had been my pleasure 10 years ago

00:11:59.970 --> 00:12:03.093
with my classmate Susan
Yisis, who some of you know,

00:12:03.970 --> 00:12:08.620
to, on behalf of the class of 1971

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:13.620
to host a luncheon celebrating
40 years of coeducation.

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:16.340
We got pushed back on that,

00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:19.580
but we did it anyway at
the Yale Club in New York.

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:21.650
And that brought together,

00:12:21.650 --> 00:12:23.280
it's my understanding for the first time

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:25.770
in about 20 to 25 years,

00:12:25.770 --> 00:12:30.550
John Wilkinson, who was here
and we saw this morning,

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:33.580
Sam, who's, I've met for the first time

00:12:33.580 --> 00:12:35.650
10 years ago, unfortunately.

00:12:35.650 --> 00:12:37.840
But also, the person who--
(laughter)

00:12:37.840 --> 00:12:38.943
- Well, I feel the same way! (laughs)

00:12:38.943 --> 00:12:40.276
- They don't have to take that.

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:42.770
When I say "unfortunately",

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:45.070
that was my first time meeting him.

00:12:45.070 --> 00:12:47.580
And, you know, I'm pleased
that I met him then,

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:50.010
but it's like, what took me so long?

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:51.770
How did it take so long
for me to meet him?

00:12:51.770 --> 00:12:53.880
But now, he can't get rid
of me, what can I say?

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:55.000
- That's good.

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:57.860
- And so, he was the one I did not know,

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:00.400
but the other person who
was at that luncheon,

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:03.340
whom I had met because she
interviewed me for Yale,

00:13:03.340 --> 00:13:05.350
and because I worked for her in this ...

00:13:05.350 --> 00:13:09.210
With her in the summer of
1973, was Elga Wasserman.

00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:12.010
And I think Diana Wasserman
is still in the room.

00:13:12.010 --> 00:13:14.049
Diana, are you still here?

00:13:14.049 --> 00:13:16.894
(applause)

00:13:16.894 --> 00:13:17.727
I know, and ...

00:13:17.727 --> 00:13:19.400
Now, that was, to me,
was the pivotal group

00:13:19.400 --> 00:13:22.290
that made coeducation happen.

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:23.740
And it was ...

00:13:23.740 --> 00:13:26.470
We heard the stories
about how things happened,

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:28.980
and we heard how we were selected,

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:32.590
and these are the people who
are just so important to us.

00:13:32.590 --> 00:13:35.730
And as we were involving John Wilkinson

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:39.657
and other people about
this particular weekend,

00:13:39.657 --> 00:13:42.580
I was a little bit hard
for us to convince Sam

00:13:42.580 --> 00:13:45.030
that he should be talking to us again.

00:13:45.030 --> 00:13:47.280
Because he's talked to us
at the cluster reunion,

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:49.560
he's talked to different reunion classes,

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:51.600
it's like what would make this different?

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:56.170
And what he was willing to
do was to do a fireside chat.

00:13:56.170 --> 00:14:00.030
He was willing and he
specifically would be honored

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:04.410
to be interviewed by Margaret Warner.

00:14:04.410 --> 00:14:05.800
So you heard the longer introduction

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:06.633
from Margaret this morning,
you know that she had worked

00:14:06.633 --> 00:14:11.633
for PBS for a number of years,
you know that she's involved

00:14:11.980 --> 00:14:13.271
with the Jackson Institute,
so I'm gonna get off the stage

00:14:13.271 --> 00:14:18.271
to just introduce the person
who we've really come to hear,

00:14:19.370 --> 00:14:22.630
and that is the key person with Elga

00:14:22.630 --> 00:14:25.080
who made coeducation happen for us,

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:28.910
who was the assistant working
with Kingman Brewster,

00:14:28.910 --> 00:14:31.040
who was the secretary of the university,

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:35.580
and who has been so involved
with many aspects of Yale.

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:38.300
And he is the voice of authority

00:14:38.300 --> 00:14:40.420
about how we came into being

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:43.460
here at Yale and is our inspiration.

00:14:43.460 --> 00:14:44.539
So, do I make up for that?

00:14:44.539 --> 00:14:46.190
- Thank you, dear.

00:14:46.190 --> 00:14:49.290
- Thank you.
(laughter)

00:14:49.290 --> 00:14:50.463
- If I believe that.

00:14:53.430 --> 00:14:54.760
- So great to be here, though.

00:14:54.760 --> 00:14:58.413
Sam, aren't you disappointed
there's not a fire?

00:14:58.413 --> 00:14:59.553
(laughter)

00:14:59.553 --> 00:15:01.350
- Well, I'm, I'm a little,
yeah, I am disappointed,

00:15:01.350 --> 00:15:03.817
but I hope there's not
a fire in the building.

00:15:03.817 --> 00:15:06.200
(laughter)
- Yeah, I do, I do, I agree.

00:15:06.200 --> 00:15:07.830
Well, Vera, thank you so much,

00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:11.770
and friends I've been with really remarked

00:15:11.770 --> 00:15:14.100
on how well-produced this has been.

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:16.260
I mean, I'm looking at some key people

00:15:16.260 --> 00:15:19.410
and I don't know how you pulled it off.

00:15:19.410 --> 00:15:21.810
It's really fantastic.

00:15:21.810 --> 00:15:24.723
So to you.
(applause)

00:15:25.616 --> 00:15:29.517
So we're going to talk for, I don't know--

00:15:30.354 --> 00:15:33.220
- A couple hours, three hours.
(laughter)

00:15:33.220 --> 00:15:36.120
- I'm trying, I don't want
to tread the same ground,

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:37.970
because everybody was there this morning,

00:15:37.970 --> 00:15:40.170
I assume, and this afternoon.

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:45.170
But Sam, you were here,
incredibly, for every event.

00:15:45.650 --> 00:15:48.640
What did we leave out?

00:15:48.640 --> 00:15:52.590
Was there something
significant about what happened

00:15:52.590 --> 00:15:55.660
and how it happened that
you'd like to highlight,

00:15:55.660 --> 00:15:59.613
that you don't think
was highlighted enough?

00:16:00.550 --> 00:16:03.030
- The first thing I would say is that

00:16:03.030 --> 00:16:05.263
in looking at anything historically,

00:16:07.539 --> 00:16:10.347
I'm not an historian, but
all great historians tell me,

00:16:10.347 --> 00:16:12.590
"You look at the context first."

00:16:12.590 --> 00:16:14.060
What was going on around.

00:16:14.060 --> 00:16:16.930
We had a wonderful panel this afternoon

00:16:16.930 --> 00:16:21.620
of people who talked about,
Bev Gage was the chair,

00:16:21.620 --> 00:16:26.620
and talked about the general context

00:16:27.130 --> 00:16:29.883
in the United States at this time.

00:16:31.580 --> 00:16:36.300
In the little book I
published about May Day, 1970,

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:41.267
I have used the phrase, "the
era of challenge to authority".

00:16:42.580 --> 00:16:47.100
And I see this period as being a period

00:16:48.770 --> 00:16:51.423
in which we've come out
of the Second World War,

00:16:54.210 --> 00:16:55.219
but we have a group of aged leaders,

00:16:55.219 --> 00:16:59.803
people who are much older.

00:17:02.210 --> 00:17:05.440
And then all of a sudden, Jack
Kennedy was elected president

00:17:05.440 --> 00:17:07.670
and to people my age then,

00:17:07.670 --> 00:17:12.260
I guess I was 25, 27,
when Kennedy was elected,

00:17:12.260 --> 00:17:13.920
it was a whole new world opening up.

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:15.037
The young were coming
forward, we were gonna get

00:17:15.037 --> 00:17:18.560
new leadership in everything we did.

00:17:18.560 --> 00:17:21.063
And then, of course, he was assassinated.

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:24.480
And then Martin Luther
King and Bobby Kennedy

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:28.920
were assassinated, and
these were our heroes,

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:31.360
depending on who we were and so on.

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:33.360
These were the great heroes.

00:17:33.360 --> 00:17:37.457
Then, Rachel Carson comes
out with the "Silent Spring",

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:40.650
and "The Feminine Mystique",

00:17:40.650 --> 00:17:44.440
and the Stonewall riots
in Greenwich Village,

00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:47.420
and the war in Vietnam.

00:17:47.420 --> 00:17:51.680
And all these challenges were out there,

00:17:51.680 --> 00:17:55.707
and young people from all
over this country were saying,

00:17:55.707 --> 00:17:58.377
"Hey wait a minute, we don't
have the right leadership.

00:17:58.377 --> 00:18:01.247
"They don't know what
they're talking about.

00:18:01.247 --> 00:18:03.550
"And we've got to change",
and just parenthetically,

00:18:03.550 --> 00:18:06.630
one of the things Kingman
Brewster said to me

00:18:06.630 --> 00:18:09.617
in this period was, "You know, Sam,

00:18:09.617 --> 00:18:10.877
"it's gonna be interesting to see

00:18:10.877 --> 00:18:15.060
"how many of these crazy
ideas are the gospel

00:18:16.187 --> 00:18:17.460
"25 years from now."

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:19.670
Well, they all became the gospel.

00:18:19.670 --> 00:18:22.220
I mean, virtually all of them.

00:18:22.220 --> 00:18:26.930
So, I'd like to remember that, nationally,

00:18:26.930 --> 00:18:31.760
this country was embroiled in a revolution

00:18:31.760 --> 00:18:34.007
in which young people were saying,

00:18:34.007 --> 00:18:36.577
"we are tired of old leadership,

00:18:36.577 --> 00:18:38.517
"and we want young leadership,

00:18:38.517 --> 00:18:41.070
"and we want this country to change."

00:18:41.070 --> 00:18:44.363
Now then, let's look at Yale itself.

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:48.390
Kingman Brewster became president in 1963

00:18:48.390 --> 00:18:52.770
when Whit Griswold died,
unhappily, of cancer.

00:18:52.770 --> 00:18:57.770
And almost immediately,
Kingman had a wonderful way

00:18:59.500 --> 00:19:02.930
of being on vacation whenever
something went wrong at Yale.

00:19:04.087 --> 00:19:06.363
And he and I had a deal that one of us ...

00:19:06.363 --> 00:19:08.690
I always said, as I
was his only assistant,

00:19:08.690 --> 00:19:10.520
and one of us had to be here.

00:19:10.520 --> 00:19:14.010
So all my conversations with
Kingman many of these years

00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:16.470
were on long-distance
telephones to the Caribbean

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:18.200
or some place like that.

00:19:18.200 --> 00:19:21.430
But the first protest at Yale was over.

00:19:21.430 --> 00:19:24.780
Not a national issue, but the failure

00:19:24.780 --> 00:19:28.050
of the philosophy
department to grant tenure

00:19:28.050 --> 00:19:31.640
to a young man named Richard Bernstein.

00:19:31.640 --> 00:19:34.580
And none of us had ever
even heard of a protest.

00:19:34.580 --> 00:19:36.710
And yet, out in front of Woodbridge Hall,

00:19:36.710 --> 00:19:38.320
people were camped all night,

00:19:38.320 --> 00:19:40.660
faculty members with their wives

00:19:40.660 --> 00:19:44.250
were serving dinner to the radicals,

00:19:44.250 --> 00:19:45.990
and we were sitting
there in Woodbridge Hall

00:19:45.990 --> 00:19:48.633
feeling a little bit
like we were under fire.

00:19:50.510 --> 00:19:51.708
This was a period in
which Yale had terrible

00:19:51.708 --> 00:19:52.541
labor relations, and that
was as much our fault

00:19:52.541 --> 00:19:57.440
as it was the union's.

00:19:57.440 --> 00:20:00.743
The union administration never
understood labor relations.

00:20:01.750 --> 00:20:03.620
We had terrible financial problems.

00:20:03.620 --> 00:20:04.794
Yale had a deficit for the entire

00:20:04.794 --> 00:20:08.723
13 years of Brewster's presidency.

00:20:10.180 --> 00:20:12.730
The endowment was not big enough,

00:20:12.730 --> 00:20:15.350
we were trying to do too much,

00:20:15.350 --> 00:20:17.980
Kingman was trying to improve the faculty

00:20:17.980 --> 00:20:20.683
and pay more, but there
was no money to do it.

00:20:22.300 --> 00:20:27.300
In 1964, Brewster invited
three very distinguished ...

00:20:27.940 --> 00:20:31.290
Excuse me, five very
distinguished scientists,

00:20:31.290 --> 00:20:34.970
to come to Yale and examine
the sciences at Yale.

00:20:34.970 --> 00:20:37.380
And the answer was, in a private memo,

00:20:37.380 --> 00:20:39.460
there were only three scientists at Yale

00:20:39.460 --> 00:20:40.613
that are worth a damn.

00:20:41.900 --> 00:20:42.733
- Out of how many?
- Imagine that.

00:20:42.733 --> 00:20:45.930
That's how bad the scientists had become.

00:20:45.930 --> 00:20:50.090
The alumni were up in arms
over a whole series of issues,

00:20:50.090 --> 00:20:53.550
and they started an organization
called Lux Et Veritas,

00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:56.653
which was dedicated to getting
Brewster out of office.

00:20:58.750 --> 00:21:01.970
There were the war issues,
the civil rights issues,

00:21:01.970 --> 00:21:05.360
and Kingman was desperately
trying to improve the faculty,

00:21:05.360 --> 00:21:07.440
both in the sciences and other fields.

00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:11.400
So, the era in which
you all were admitted,

00:21:11.400 --> 00:21:14.870
there was a whole lot of
things going on outside.

00:21:14.870 --> 00:21:17.590
And those things were both consuming

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:21.700
the minds and the
thoughts of young people,

00:21:21.700 --> 00:21:23.030
but they were also the things

00:21:23.030 --> 00:21:27.630
I've just mentioned at Yale
were consuming our lives.

00:21:27.630 --> 00:21:30.590
So much as admitting you

00:21:30.590 --> 00:21:33.510
was the most exciting thing that we did,

00:21:33.510 --> 00:21:34.904
we had a whole lot of other work to do.

00:21:34.904 --> 00:21:35.737
(laughter)

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:38.523
- You have opined in other
settings that a major ...

00:21:38.523 --> 00:21:43.320
And I don't know if you
said a great university,

00:21:43.320 --> 00:21:47.600
but changes rarely come in response

00:21:47.600 --> 00:21:50.570
to internal pressures, but external.

00:21:50.570 --> 00:21:52.070
Yet, today we heard about ...

00:21:52.070 --> 00:21:52.903
That faculty were unhappy because the men

00:21:52.903 --> 00:21:57.620
weren't as accomplished as they'd like.

00:21:58.640 --> 00:22:01.720
And, you know, we hear
that the male students,

00:22:01.720 --> 00:22:03.680
the undergrads, were also agitated.

00:22:03.680 --> 00:22:07.670
But do you think your paradigm holds?

00:22:07.670 --> 00:22:12.053
- Yes, I would still argue
that, if there had not been

00:22:14.920 --> 00:22:18.083
a societal change coming
in the role of women,

00:22:20.398 --> 00:22:22.280
that Yale would never
have gone coeducation,

00:22:22.280 --> 00:22:24.460
or at least not at that time.

00:22:24.460 --> 00:22:27.380
But the pressures of the societal change

00:22:27.380 --> 00:22:30.330
were being felt by Princeton,
and by Yale, and by Dartmouth,

00:22:30.330 --> 00:22:32.600
which were the only three
Ivy League institutions

00:22:32.600 --> 00:22:35.513
that had no women involved with them.

00:22:37.160 --> 00:22:38.407
And I think that the ...

00:22:38.407 --> 00:22:43.407
You know, there were a couple of students,

00:22:43.960 --> 00:22:45.270
and Lanny Davis, who
was chairman of the news

00:22:45.270 --> 00:22:50.270
in 1966, '67, I think.

00:22:51.100 --> 00:22:52.850
He wrote an editorial every single day,

00:22:52.850 --> 00:22:54.770
calling us the dumbest administrators

00:22:54.770 --> 00:22:55.808
in the entire universe, world,

00:22:55.808 --> 00:22:58.433
because we weren't admitting women.

00:22:59.310 --> 00:23:00.743
So he had an impact.

00:23:01.890 --> 00:23:05.013
But I do not think that the ...

00:23:06.200 --> 00:23:09.670
Someone said today, and it
may have been Nancy Malkiel,

00:23:09.670 --> 00:23:11.810
that we were competitive with Princeton.

00:23:11.810 --> 00:23:14.170
I actually don't remember being ...

00:23:14.170 --> 00:23:15.993
Feeling competitive with Princeton.

00:23:17.850 --> 00:23:20.900
I thought we were competitive with Harvard

00:23:20.900 --> 00:23:22.873
because they had Radcliffe and so on.

00:23:25.640 --> 00:23:29.170
I think the other thing that ...

00:23:29.170 --> 00:23:32.500
The Vassar project,
which did not work out,

00:23:32.500 --> 00:23:33.910
had gotten Yale kind of geared up.

00:23:33.910 --> 00:23:35.570
- Mm-hm.

00:23:35.570 --> 00:23:39.883
- So, I still think it was
more external than internal.

00:23:41.140 --> 00:23:44.600
- Even though, so you're saying you think

00:23:44.600 --> 00:23:47.280
that the feminist movement
was powerful enough

00:23:47.280 --> 00:23:50.490
at that early stage to create a context

00:23:50.490 --> 00:23:52.307
that kind of pushed--
- Yeah.

00:23:52.307 --> 00:23:54.450
Let me tell you a wonderful story.

00:23:54.450 --> 00:23:56.760
There was one morning when I realized

00:23:56.760 --> 00:23:58.026
that I had scheduled an appointment

00:23:58.026 --> 00:23:58.859
for Kingman to see the feminine ...

00:23:58.859 --> 00:24:03.859
Who wrote "The Feminine Mystique"?

00:24:04.310 --> 00:24:05.143
- Betty Friedan.

00:24:05.143 --> 00:24:05.976
- Betty Friedan.

00:24:07.700 --> 00:24:12.240
I'd never met Betty Friedan,
but the only appointment open

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:13.810
was eight o'clock in the morning.

00:24:13.810 --> 00:24:16.220
It was clear when Ms. Friedan walked in

00:24:16.220 --> 00:24:17.521
that she had never been up before 11:30.

00:24:17.521 --> 00:24:18.354
(laughter)

00:24:21.150 --> 00:24:24.020
It was also clear that
she was very unhappy

00:24:24.020 --> 00:24:26.790
to be called at eight
o'clock in the morning

00:24:26.790 --> 00:24:27.950
to see the president of Yale.

00:24:27.950 --> 00:24:31.680
So, she went in and she went out,

00:24:31.680 --> 00:24:32.930
and that was a day in which I knew

00:24:32.930 --> 00:24:33.763
I had to recommend to the president ...

00:24:33.763 --> 00:24:37.610
We didn't have search committees,

00:24:37.610 --> 00:24:40.060
Peter, in those days, we just did things.

00:24:40.060 --> 00:24:44.630
I had to recommend the first
woman master of a college,

00:24:44.630 --> 00:24:48.630
who was Kitty Lustman,
Katherine Findling-Lustman.

00:24:48.630 --> 00:24:50.080
So a little later in the ...
(applause)

00:24:50.080 --> 00:24:52.680
Yeah, you're right, great woman.

00:24:52.680 --> 00:24:54.610
A little later in the morning,
I went in to see Kingman,

00:24:54.610 --> 00:24:57.707
and I said, "I'd like to
suggest that you appoint

00:24:57.707 --> 00:25:01.420
"Kitty Lustman as the master
of Davenport College."

00:25:01.420 --> 00:25:03.670
He got up, went to the
window and looked out,

00:25:04.610 --> 00:25:07.157
nothing said, he looked out,

00:25:07.157 --> 00:25:09.355
and he turned around, he
said, "Is she a feminist?"

00:25:09.355 --> 00:25:10.188
(laughter)

00:25:10.188 --> 00:25:14.470
So ... (laughs)

00:25:14.470 --> 00:25:15.690
- Well then, this is ...

00:25:15.690 --> 00:25:18.960
I, we really wanna hear
about Kingman Brewster

00:25:18.960 --> 00:25:20.438
because this morning, I mean,

00:25:20.438 --> 00:25:24.713
I wrote in my notes, mystery man.

00:25:25.689 --> 00:25:28.403
What, you were very close to him.

00:25:28.403 --> 00:25:30.860
I mean, we heard John Wilkinson talk about

00:25:30.860 --> 00:25:33.080
that he was under so many pressures:

00:25:33.080 --> 00:25:36.130
money, the alums, and so forth,

00:25:36.130 --> 00:25:39.960
but what do you think was
really at the heart of ...

00:25:39.960 --> 00:25:43.170
And Kurt Schmoke said
there was this committee,

00:25:43.170 --> 00:25:44.610
committee on coeducation,

00:25:44.610 --> 00:25:47.320
and they'd actually prepare reports

00:25:47.320 --> 00:25:49.590
about what was missing here, there,

00:25:49.590 --> 00:25:51.990
and hadn't been done, and
he wouldn't read them.

00:25:54.442 --> 00:25:57.377
So, fill us in on this character.

00:26:00.070 --> 00:26:03.030
- Kingman Brewster was born in the 1920s.

00:26:03.030 --> 00:26:06.870
He came from a very old New
England family but not ...

00:26:06.870 --> 00:26:08.150
People thought he was very rich.

00:26:08.150 --> 00:26:10.810
He, actually, was not at all well to do.

00:26:10.810 --> 00:26:15.593
Indeed, that was a problem
for him and his entire career.

00:26:17.160 --> 00:26:19.310
But he came from an
old New England family.

00:26:20.270 --> 00:26:24.130
His father was a ne'er-do-well man

00:26:24.130 --> 00:26:27.210
who constantly was gonna make millions

00:26:27.210 --> 00:26:28.663
but never quite did it.

00:26:29.660 --> 00:26:31.720
His parents were divorced early.

00:26:31.720 --> 00:26:35.480
His mother, Florence Valentine,

00:26:35.480 --> 00:26:40.120
married a professor of music
at the Harvard Music School

00:26:40.120 --> 00:26:43.410
and was an absolutely magnificent woman

00:26:43.410 --> 00:26:44.763
way ahead of her time.

00:26:45.810 --> 00:26:47.440
She wasn't a feminist,

00:26:47.440 --> 00:26:50.053
but she was a feminist
in the old-world sense.

00:26:53.300 --> 00:26:57.900
Kingman was a radical as an undergraduate.

00:26:57.900 --> 00:27:01.534
He belonged to the
American First Movement,

00:27:01.534 --> 00:27:02.367
which was opposed to the ...

00:27:02.367 --> 00:27:03.580
Going into the Second World War,

00:27:03.580 --> 00:27:07.360
and he was, he and Charles
Lindbergh were the two ...

00:27:07.360 --> 00:27:08.730
He was the undergraduate leader

00:27:08.730 --> 00:27:10.630
and Lindbergh was the national leader.

00:27:11.670 --> 00:27:14.243
So, he was very radical
as an undergraduate.

00:27:15.080 --> 00:27:20.080
He came to Yale and
graduated, and then ...

00:27:20.690 --> 00:27:24.270
No, he came to, he went
into the service first.

00:27:24.270 --> 00:27:27.550
No, I'm sorry, he graduated
and went into the service.

00:27:27.550 --> 00:27:29.890
And immediately under the service,

00:27:29.890 --> 00:27:31.290
when the country went into war,

00:27:31.290 --> 00:27:33.483
he just did a total about-face.

00:27:35.420 --> 00:27:38.773
Mary Louise Brewster,
born in the same era,

00:27:40.530 --> 00:27:45.530
went to Vassar, but left to
marry him during the war time.

00:27:45.730 --> 00:27:49.663
But she was also a trustee
advisor afterwards.

00:27:50.790 --> 00:27:53.750
They both believed that
single-sex education

00:27:53.750 --> 00:27:56.050
was the right way to go.

00:27:56.050 --> 00:27:57.256
And there was no doubt
in their minds that,

00:27:57.256 --> 00:27:59.880
from an educational point of view,

00:27:59.880 --> 00:28:02.000
this was the right thing to do.

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:04.463
- What was the reasoning
of that, behind that?

00:28:06.660 --> 00:28:07.879
- Margaret, I think, I'm not
sure I ever got to the bottom

00:28:07.879 --> 00:28:12.879
of that, but I think
they just believed it,

00:28:13.390 --> 00:28:16.340
almost in a sense that we brought ...

00:28:16.340 --> 00:28:17.391
Been brought up to believe
that having a college education

00:28:17.391 --> 00:28:20.090
is a rather good thing.

00:28:20.090 --> 00:28:21.570
I think they just believed that

00:28:21.570 --> 00:28:23.270
single-sex education was right.

00:28:23.270 --> 00:28:24.466
Now, Kingman had a little
bit of the Old Blue,

00:28:24.466 --> 00:28:25.299
you know, "Stover at
Yale", the kind of Old Blue

00:28:25.299 --> 00:28:30.299
that this is a man's world and so on.

00:28:34.210 --> 00:28:35.510
So, I think that was part of it,

00:28:35.510 --> 00:28:40.510
but the second thing I wanna
say about Kingman is that

00:28:42.550 --> 00:28:45.223
he was the quintessential pragmatic.

00:28:46.390 --> 00:28:49.410
And I will never forget the night

00:28:49.410 --> 00:28:50.753
after Vassar had said they
didn't wanna come, we were ...

00:28:50.753 --> 00:28:55.040
Charles Taylor, who was
the long-time provost,

00:28:55.040 --> 00:28:58.780
and he and I were contemporaries, really.

00:28:58.780 --> 00:29:01.310
We both were in college in the '50s,

00:29:01.310 --> 00:29:03.670
and we both wanted to
have real coeducation.

00:29:03.670 --> 00:29:07.520
And Charlie and I were
sitting talking to Kingman,

00:29:07.520 --> 00:29:10.290
and the drumbeat was going,

00:29:10.290 --> 00:29:13.757
and I think it was Charlie who
turned to Kingman and said,

00:29:13.757 --> 00:29:15.507
"Look, Kingman, there's
a snowball up there,

00:29:15.507 --> 00:29:19.077
"and it's coming down the
hill, and you have a choice.

00:29:19.077 --> 00:29:22.557
"You can get under it and
we'll never see you again,

00:29:22.557 --> 00:29:27.490
"or you can step aside and
try to help direct it."

00:29:27.490 --> 00:29:30.500
And the light went on with
Brewster, just like that.

00:29:30.500 --> 00:29:32.570
And he said, "You're telling
we have to be coeducation?"

00:29:32.570 --> 00:29:33.630
And we said, "Yes."

00:29:33.630 --> 00:29:37.560
And he said, "Okay, let's do it."

00:29:37.560 --> 00:29:40.180
But he was very pragmatic.

00:29:40.180 --> 00:29:45.180
And he didn't compromise
basic ethical principles,

00:29:46.840 --> 00:29:48.015
but he knew enough to see when
things were changing that ...

00:29:48.015 --> 00:29:53.015
When we decided, originally,
that all the women

00:29:53.230 --> 00:29:55.565
were going to live in one college,

00:29:55.565 --> 00:29:57.320
and it was gonna be Trumbull College.

00:29:57.320 --> 00:29:58.410
And he went to the meeting,

00:29:58.410 --> 00:29:59.980
John Wilkinson referred him to it,

00:29:59.980 --> 00:30:01.550
he went to a meeting in Trumbull College.

00:30:01.550 --> 00:30:04.460
The only time I ever worried
about Kingman's welfare,

00:30:04.460 --> 00:30:08.140
physical welfare, because
they'd built a stage

00:30:08.140 --> 00:30:11.470
that was sort of, little
tiny thing like this

00:30:11.470 --> 00:30:13.880
but it looked like it was
gonna collapse anyway.

00:30:13.880 --> 00:30:16.013
And the students were really angry.

00:30:18.591 --> 00:30:20.450
And he heard it all,
he listened to it all,

00:30:20.450 --> 00:30:21.747
and as we're walking out, he said,

00:30:21.747 --> 00:30:22.702
"I think we just got the message."

00:30:22.702 --> 00:30:23.535
(laughter)

00:30:25.090 --> 00:30:28.523
And so, we changed, and I
think it was a good idea.

00:30:29.690 --> 00:30:33.340
- We, most of us or many of us
saw this film last night ...

00:30:33.340 --> 00:30:35.680
We saw several, but
there was a terrific one

00:30:35.680 --> 00:30:37.350
and I don't know who produced it,

00:30:37.350 --> 00:30:39.110
I'm sorry if that person's here.

00:30:39.110 --> 00:30:43.430
I think it might have been
made for the 1989 reunion.

00:30:43.430 --> 00:30:44.448
In any event, a much ...

00:30:44.448 --> 00:30:49.448
Should I say, a much younger
Mr. Chauncey is talking.

00:30:51.730 --> 00:30:55.570
And you described, I
couldn't really take notes,

00:30:55.570 --> 00:30:58.680
but this very, to me, strange,

00:30:58.680 --> 00:31:00.400
attitude about the intellectual

00:31:00.400 --> 00:31:04.800
capabilities of women that was prevalent.

00:31:04.800 --> 00:31:09.520
You talked about that some men thought

00:31:09.520 --> 00:31:10.810
women just weren't up to it,

00:31:10.810 --> 00:31:12.350
to the intellectual jousting.

00:31:12.350 --> 00:31:14.060
Tell us about that.

00:31:14.060 --> 00:31:17.480
- I honestly don't remember
that particular movie,

00:31:17.480 --> 00:31:19.370
so I'm not sure I can--

00:31:19.370 --> 00:31:21.290
- No, but I mean just the subject.

00:31:21.290 --> 00:31:22.490
- Pick up on that, well.

00:31:23.870 --> 00:31:28.763
There were all kinds of
different people at Yale.

00:31:34.538 --> 00:31:36.130
And, I remember a man name George Pierson.

00:31:36.130 --> 00:31:37.950
That name will mean something to Judy

00:31:37.950 --> 00:31:41.053
and some of the rest of you,
the great Yale historian.

00:31:42.840 --> 00:31:45.440
George Pierson was commissioned

00:31:45.440 --> 00:31:47.370
by first Whit Griswold and then Kingman

00:31:47.370 --> 00:31:50.560
to create the best history
department in the United States.

00:31:50.560 --> 00:31:52.703
This was about 1961.

00:31:53.640 --> 00:31:56.143
And George went about doing it.

00:31:57.520 --> 00:31:59.373
And he had two prejudices.

00:32:00.620 --> 00:32:02.610
No women in Yale College,

00:32:02.610 --> 00:32:04.483
and no Jewish people in Yale College.

00:32:06.090 --> 00:32:08.973
So who were his first two hires?

00:32:10.330 --> 00:32:14.233
John Blum, a Jewish man from MIT,

00:32:15.330 --> 00:32:19.713
and Mary Wright, a professor
of Chinese History.

00:32:22.120 --> 00:32:25.300
And I thought to myself,
George has been converted.

00:32:25.300 --> 00:32:27.383
He's come around, he sees the light.

00:32:29.310 --> 00:32:32.400
In one, one of you, maybe
someone in this room

00:32:32.400 --> 00:32:35.693
came to see me in the first term,

00:32:36.570 --> 00:32:40.280
and said that they had been
in George Pierson's class

00:32:40.280 --> 00:32:43.403
and that she was one of two women,

00:32:44.330 --> 00:32:47.910
and that George had an oust
at the beginning of this term

00:32:48.930 --> 00:32:51.860
that there would be, it
was a 50-minute class,

00:32:51.860 --> 00:32:54.860
and that he would talk for 35 minutes

00:32:54.860 --> 00:32:56.190
and then we'd have discussion

00:32:56.190 --> 00:32:57.960
and then five minutes would be assigned

00:32:57.960 --> 00:32:59.460
for the women's point of view.

00:33:00.870 --> 00:33:04.080
So, you had this really complex situation

00:33:04.080 --> 00:33:08.130
of people who, on the
one hand, got the message

00:33:08.130 --> 00:33:10.060
about what was gonna go on,

00:33:10.060 --> 00:33:13.230
and on the other hand,
they would retreat back,

00:33:13.230 --> 00:33:16.190
they couldn't get
themselves out of the past.

00:33:16.190 --> 00:33:19.300
And I think maybe that's
what I was talking about

00:33:19.300 --> 00:33:22.440
in this event, because there were so many

00:33:22.440 --> 00:33:26.603
different points of view about
why we were taking women.

00:33:27.490 --> 00:33:30.730
And they ranged from the admissions office

00:33:30.730 --> 00:33:33.860
said we can't get any men
unless you admit women,

00:33:33.860 --> 00:33:38.483
the societal pressure, business,

00:33:40.390 --> 00:33:42.160
there were some people actually believed

00:33:42.160 --> 00:33:44.730
that Yale had an obligation to educate

00:33:44.730 --> 00:33:46.410
the other 50% of the populationm

00:33:46.410 --> 00:33:48.940
and that might be a good idea.

00:33:48.940 --> 00:33:53.100
But there was no prevailing philosophy.

00:33:53.100 --> 00:33:56.110
This all happens so fast,
you've got to remember

00:33:56.110 --> 00:34:01.110
that the corporation
voted in November of '68,

00:34:01.130 --> 00:34:04.000
and Elga and I had to get everything done

00:34:04.000 --> 00:34:07.000
by August of the next year.

00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:10.460
The admissions, the
facilities, everything.

00:34:10.460 --> 00:34:14.230
And so I wanted, I'm not proud of this,

00:34:14.230 --> 00:34:15.260
but I wanna underscore the fact

00:34:15.260 --> 00:34:18.900
there was no great underlying philosophy.

00:34:18.900 --> 00:34:21.400
Nancy again talked about the fact

00:34:21.400 --> 00:34:23.720
that maybe we'd kinda rushed
into this too quickly,

00:34:23.720 --> 00:34:25.607
and the answer was we did,

00:34:25.607 --> 00:34:26.980
and you rushed in too quickly,
and it was a good idea.

00:34:26.980 --> 00:34:27.813
(laughter)

00:34:29.660 --> 00:34:34.660
But, I think that was the ...
(applause)

00:34:35.100 --> 00:34:37.020
I think the fact that you must not look

00:34:37.020 --> 00:34:39.823
for a philosophy in this event.

00:34:39.823 --> 00:34:41.970
It didn't exist.

00:34:41.970 --> 00:34:44.500
A lot of people had a
lot of different reasons

00:34:44.500 --> 00:34:46.090
why women were coming.

00:34:46.090 --> 00:34:50.150
The great, and we'll, I know
you want to come back to this,

00:34:50.150 --> 00:34:53.340
but the great anchor was Elga Wasserman.

00:34:53.340 --> 00:34:57.160
Because Elga ...
(applause)

00:34:57.160 --> 00:35:00.680
Elga had the capacity, on the one hand,

00:35:00.680 --> 00:35:05.320
to be a damn good administrator,
and to get things done.

00:35:05.320 --> 00:35:08.823
On the other hand, as I
said at Mory's last night,

00:35:09.890 --> 00:35:12.323
Elga was a pioneer, and she had a vision.

00:35:13.660 --> 00:35:18.140
And pioneers who have visions
never quite accomplish

00:35:18.140 --> 00:35:19.883
everything they wanna get done.

00:35:21.230 --> 00:35:26.027
But she had a vision in
which Yale would have

00:35:27.590 --> 00:35:30.500
on the faculty, in the student body,

00:35:30.500 --> 00:35:35.500
in the administration,
50% men and 50% women

00:35:36.120 --> 00:35:38.253
at every level and every rank.

00:35:39.370 --> 00:35:43.680
And that was what true coeducation meant.

00:35:43.680 --> 00:35:46.330
To Elga, it wasn't just
admitting undergraduate women,

00:35:46.330 --> 00:35:48.523
it was the whole damn ball game.

00:35:49.570 --> 00:35:53.490
So, if we ever had any
philosophy that we adopted later,

00:35:53.490 --> 00:35:55.720
it was Elga's philosophy.

00:35:55.720 --> 00:35:57.830
And she was the one
who brought that to us.

00:35:57.830 --> 00:35:59.042
It didn't work, we did not ...

00:35:59.042 --> 00:36:04.042
We haven't yet quite succeeded
in achieving Elga's goal,

00:36:05.340 --> 00:36:10.080
but I think that that's
the way it turned out.

00:36:10.080 --> 00:36:14.423
- And did she, how did she
feel about being sidelined?

00:36:15.941 --> 00:36:18.440
I mean, it sounds like
President Brewster doesn't,

00:36:18.440 --> 00:36:19.686
didn't, I mean, on the one
hand, he depended on her.

00:36:19.686 --> 00:36:23.610
She was doing half the job you were,

00:36:23.610 --> 00:36:27.960
but this business about not
giving her a real position,

00:36:27.960 --> 00:36:30.370
she was an assistant, what was that about?

00:36:30.370 --> 00:36:32.213
- Well, let me tell you two things.

00:36:36.580 --> 00:36:39.430
I'll give you an analogy,
because I think some of you

00:36:39.430 --> 00:36:40.530
may understand it better,

00:36:40.530 --> 00:36:42.440
and then I'll bring it back to Elga.

00:36:42.440 --> 00:36:44.513
A lot of you have heard of Bill Coffin,

00:36:45.490 --> 00:36:48.020
the great chaplain at the time.

00:36:48.020 --> 00:36:49.253
And Bill was a ...
(applause)

00:36:49.253 --> 00:36:53.380
Bill was, himself, a revolutionary

00:36:53.380 --> 00:36:55.410
in the best sense of the word.

00:36:55.410 --> 00:36:59.100
And passionate in his
believe about civil rights,

00:36:59.100 --> 00:37:01.453
and the draft and the Vietnam War.

00:37:02.800 --> 00:37:06.410
Kingman and Bill Coffin fought like tigers

00:37:06.410 --> 00:37:09.500
day after day after
day but you could never

00:37:09.500 --> 00:37:11.133
have found two better friends.

00:37:12.300 --> 00:37:17.300
They fought all the time
because Bill was the idealist,

00:37:19.430 --> 00:37:22.240
Kingman had to make the damn place run.

00:37:22.240 --> 00:37:24.170
And so, they were clashing with each other

00:37:24.170 --> 00:37:27.850
every day of the week, and
yet they loved each other.

00:37:27.850 --> 00:37:30.570
Now, I won't say that Elga
and Kingman loved each other,

00:37:30.570 --> 00:37:33.490
but they deeply respected each other.

00:37:33.490 --> 00:37:38.220
And yet, I think that, as it turns out,

00:37:38.220 --> 00:37:40.920
I think it's very
healthy for a university,

00:37:40.920 --> 00:37:44.821
because anyone who's president, I mean,

00:37:44.821 --> 00:37:45.654
this man knows better than anybody

00:37:45.654 --> 00:37:48.710
in the world as president.

00:37:48.710 --> 00:37:53.710
Constantly, visionaries and missionaries

00:37:53.760 --> 00:37:56.927
are coming and saying,
"You help me get ...

00:37:56.927 --> 00:37:58.937
"Make my thing work."

00:38:00.160 --> 00:38:01.353
And how many dozens come in

00:38:01.353 --> 00:38:02.186
to a president of university every year?

00:38:02.186 --> 00:38:07.186
I don't know, but there's
a whole lot of them,

00:38:07.320 --> 00:38:08.870
and they want money, and they want help,

00:38:08.870 --> 00:38:10.610
and they want everything.

00:38:10.610 --> 00:38:12.950
The president favors certain ones of those

00:38:12.950 --> 00:38:16.210
because he or she thinks it's a good idea.

00:38:16.210 --> 00:38:17.810
And Kingman ultimately believed

00:38:17.810 --> 00:38:19.940
that coeducation was a good idea.

00:38:19.940 --> 00:38:24.940
But Elga never, as Bill never did,

00:38:25.740 --> 00:38:29.750
Elga never stopped
believing in her principle

00:38:29.750 --> 00:38:33.333
and her goal for this institution.

00:38:34.350 --> 00:38:38.740
And Kingman never stopped
trying to make it work.

00:38:38.740 --> 00:38:43.740
And so, the conflict, which
is perhaps overplayed as a ...

00:38:47.390 --> 00:38:51.473
As an angry, or I don't
think that ever existed.

00:38:51.473 --> 00:38:55.840
Now, as to her title, that
was a very separate issue.

00:38:55.840 --> 00:38:59.420
Elga wanted to be the associate dean

00:38:59.420 --> 00:39:01.433
or the deputy dean of Yale College.

00:39:06.460 --> 00:39:10.200
The dean of Yale College
did not want to be told

00:39:10.200 --> 00:39:13.040
by the president who was going to be

00:39:13.040 --> 00:39:15.713
his deputies or his assistants.

00:39:16.970 --> 00:39:17.803
- And who was the dean?

00:39:17.803 --> 00:39:21.770
- George May, professor
of French, wonderful man.

00:39:21.770 --> 00:39:24.570
But, he would not exceed to being told

00:39:24.570 --> 00:39:27.120
from Woodbridge Hall who
was gonna be in his office.

00:39:27.120 --> 00:39:29.120
He felt that was his prerogative

00:39:29.120 --> 00:39:31.800
and that he was going
to appoint the people.

00:39:31.800 --> 00:39:33.760
And it may have been a mistake for us

00:39:33.760 --> 00:39:38.220
to go to George and ask him
to make this appointment.

00:39:38.220 --> 00:39:39.417
Because maybe, if he had thought it up,

00:39:39.417 --> 00:39:41.313
it might have happened.

00:39:42.800 --> 00:39:45.530
So, I think that's a separate issue

00:39:45.530 --> 00:39:49.223
which gets into the politics
of how universities operate.

00:39:51.170 --> 00:39:53.900
- So, it was not anything deeper

00:39:53.900 --> 00:39:57.770
that came and didn't
want someone so strong?

00:39:57.770 --> 00:39:59.620
- I happen to have actually felt,

00:39:59.620 --> 00:40:01.230
and I talked to Elga about this,

00:40:01.230 --> 00:40:02.760
I happened to have actually thought

00:40:02.760 --> 00:40:07.220
she had more power as an
assistant to the president

00:40:07.220 --> 00:40:08.160
than she would have had

00:40:08.160 --> 00:40:10.530
as an associate dean of Yale College.

00:40:10.530 --> 00:40:13.220
I just thoughtm in the scheme of things,

00:40:13.220 --> 00:40:14.470
it might have been a better ...

00:40:14.470 --> 00:40:16.853
But, her view, and I, it was ...

00:40:17.960 --> 00:40:21.550
Based in her feeling about the
academic side, in many ways,

00:40:21.550 --> 00:40:22.670
that being an associate dean

00:40:22.670 --> 00:40:25.110
was a more important academic position

00:40:25.110 --> 00:40:27.143
as opposed to being an administrator.

00:40:28.060 --> 00:40:29.204
- So, I know other people ...

00:40:29.204 --> 00:40:30.037
I asked this this morning,
and it got a laugh,

00:40:30.037 --> 00:40:35.037
but I'd really like to know.

00:40:36.470 --> 00:40:39.910
How did it happen that
even the most basic,

00:40:39.910 --> 00:40:42.670
like having bathrooms in the classroom,

00:40:42.670 --> 00:40:46.028
buildings, was overlooked?

00:40:46.028 --> 00:40:49.028
(laughter)

00:40:52.270 --> 00:40:53.340
- I have to tell you a story first.

00:40:53.340 --> 00:40:54.890
(laughter)

00:40:54.890 --> 00:40:55.893
- Stories are great.

00:40:56.750 --> 00:40:58.760
- There was a registrar at Yale College.

00:40:58.760 --> 00:41:00.580
His name was Ronald C. Marsh.

00:41:00.580 --> 00:41:03.173
He graduated in the class of 1917,

00:41:04.485 --> 00:41:08.163
and he was registrar from
1917 until God knows when.

00:41:09.060 --> 00:41:12.450
He was in the Strathcona,

00:41:12.450 --> 00:41:15.030
and he had 13 ladies who worked for him,

00:41:15.030 --> 00:41:17.943
and they all sat there with
pencils, recording grades.

00:41:18.930 --> 00:41:21.900
Ronald always wore the same attire.

00:41:21.900 --> 00:41:24.560
Not, I mean, he changed,
got it cleaned and stuff,

00:41:24.560 --> 00:41:28.010
but he always wore a very heavy gray suit,

00:41:28.010 --> 00:41:31.473
this was in the year
of no air conditioning.

00:41:32.340 --> 00:41:36.873
He wore a stiff detachable white
collar and a Yale blue tie.

00:41:38.590 --> 00:41:40.310
One of the 13 ladies who worked

00:41:40.310 --> 00:41:43.400
in his office was Mrs. Marsh.

00:41:43.400 --> 00:41:45.420
We were not allowed to call her Vesta.

00:41:45.420 --> 00:41:47.480
She used to be called Mrs. Marsh.

00:41:47.480 --> 00:41:52.480
Ronald had on his desk
a telephone and a key.

00:41:54.580 --> 00:41:58.860
And each day, if a lady wished
to make a telephone call,

00:41:58.860 --> 00:41:59.980
she had to stand right next to Ronald

00:41:59.980 --> 00:42:02.183
and make the telephone call.

00:42:03.370 --> 00:42:06.100
And if she wanted to
go to the ladies room,

00:42:06.100 --> 00:42:09.554
there was one in SSS
then on the eighth floor,

00:42:09.554 --> 00:42:11.810
(laughter)
she had to take the key

00:42:11.810 --> 00:42:15.130
from Ronald, and Ronald
only allowed them to go

00:42:15.130 --> 00:42:17.430
once in the morning and
once in the afternoon.

00:42:20.391 --> 00:42:22.259
- Wow.
- Bathrooms were a problem.

00:42:22.259 --> 00:42:24.177
(laughter)
- It seems to have been.

00:42:25.290 --> 00:42:26.603
For generations.

00:42:29.350 --> 00:42:34.271
- We did, we worried, first,
about these sorts of things

00:42:34.271 --> 00:42:36.970
in the places where the
women were going to live.

00:42:36.970 --> 00:42:39.400
And make sure that those were all working.

00:42:39.400 --> 00:42:40.630
And there was a famous story

00:42:40.630 --> 00:42:42.473
about my wanting to take the bath,

00:42:43.402 --> 00:42:45.510
the showers out of
Vanderbilt and put bathtubs.

00:42:45.510 --> 00:42:48.730
because my five sisters had
said women wanted bathtubs.

00:42:48.730 --> 00:42:51.660
And then the women said
no, leave them in there.

00:42:51.660 --> 00:42:56.520
But it, there were so
many buildings at Yale

00:42:56.520 --> 00:42:59.480
that we just, in the seven months

00:42:59.480 --> 00:43:00.780
that we had to do everything else,

00:43:00.780 --> 00:43:03.420
we did not have time, we did,

00:43:03.420 --> 00:43:05.630
somebody went around and
looked in every place,

00:43:05.630 --> 00:43:08.000
but we just didn't get it done.

00:43:08.000 --> 00:43:10.940
It was too, we had too
little time to get it done.

00:43:10.940 --> 00:43:15.010
Remember, one nice person who was here

00:43:15.010 --> 00:43:20.010
saw me earlier, reminded me
that she lives in New York,

00:43:20.370 --> 00:43:25.370
and she said, "You know,
to have all the permits

00:43:26.847 --> 00:43:30.870
"to renovate your kitchen in New York City

00:43:31.857 --> 00:43:35.527
"takes longer than we had

00:43:35.527 --> 00:43:38.107
"in order to get all this
done for all the women."

00:43:40.070 --> 00:43:41.070
- Charlie's remarkable.

00:43:41.070 --> 00:43:42.363
- It's, we're guilty.

00:43:43.430 --> 00:43:45.380
- But all of us who've done renovations

00:43:45.380 --> 00:43:47.580
can sympathize with that.

00:43:47.580 --> 00:43:49.750
But I want to get to
questions from the audience,

00:43:49.750 --> 00:43:54.390
but sitting back and reflecting now,

00:43:54.390 --> 00:43:57.453
one, how momentous do you
think it was, coeducation?

00:43:59.120 --> 00:44:03.250
And in the sort of roster of really urgent

00:44:03.250 --> 00:44:05.710
emergencies that you had to deal with,

00:44:05.710 --> 00:44:09.220
where does this rank, and
please don't flatter us,

00:44:09.220 --> 00:44:12.320
but where does that having women here

00:44:12.320 --> 00:44:17.320
rank in what you've achieved
and the greatest legacy?

00:44:18.610 --> 00:44:21.240
- Well, I'll start one step higher.

00:44:21.240 --> 00:44:23.520
Many of you may know Professor Jay Gitlin

00:44:23.520 --> 00:44:24.682
in the History Department,
who's really our,

00:44:24.682 --> 00:44:25.515
sort of, university historian.

00:44:25.515 --> 00:44:29.820
He's not officially that,

00:44:29.820 --> 00:44:32.300
but he knows a lot about Yale history.

00:44:32.300 --> 00:44:35.500
And he teaches as class that
relates to Yale history,

00:44:35.500 --> 00:44:38.270
which I attend, and he
and I have a big argument

00:44:38.270 --> 00:44:41.010
about when did the old
Yale become the new Yale?

00:44:41.010 --> 00:44:45.530
And my argument is that
the period of challenge

00:44:45.530 --> 00:44:48.550
to authority, the period
of your coming to Yale,

00:44:48.550 --> 00:44:51.600
is the period in which the
old Yale becomes the new Yale,

00:44:51.600 --> 00:44:56.600
because it involves
women, minority students,

00:44:58.110 --> 00:45:01.710
it involves the period
in which the Yale faculty

00:45:01.710 --> 00:45:05.510
recognizes that there are really good PhDs

00:45:05.510 --> 00:45:08.010
other than Yale PhDs.
(laughter)

00:45:08.010 --> 00:45:11.290
And we begin having men, and later women,

00:45:11.290 --> 00:45:13.160
come from the great state universities

00:45:13.160 --> 00:45:14.773
where they always taught women.

00:45:17.350 --> 00:45:19.660
And so, I think this era,

00:45:19.660 --> 00:45:23.530
and I won't pick a particular part of it,

00:45:23.530 --> 00:45:24.363
but to say this is the time

00:45:24.363 --> 00:45:28.780
that the old Yale became the new Yale.

00:45:28.780 --> 00:45:33.780
As to the, sort of relative,
maybe difficult year, so on.

00:45:36.670 --> 00:45:39.550
We worked much further ahead in planning

00:45:39.550 --> 00:45:41.400
for the admission of minority students,

00:45:41.400 --> 00:45:43.940
that is Arthur Howe had actually started

00:45:43.940 --> 00:45:48.940
a program in 1956 or '57
called the ABC Program.

00:45:49.240 --> 00:45:51.010
And we began planning.

00:45:51.010 --> 00:45:55.180
And so, by the time a larger
group of black students came,

00:45:55.180 --> 00:45:59.250
we'd already developed the
Afro-American Studies department,

00:45:59.250 --> 00:46:03.830
we'd already developed the
Afro-American Cultural Center,

00:46:03.830 --> 00:46:06.180
and so, I think that was the one

00:46:06.180 --> 00:46:08.180
that we probably did the best job on.

00:46:08.180 --> 00:46:09.810
I'm not saying it was a perfect job.

00:46:09.810 --> 00:46:11.340
But I think we did the best job.

00:46:11.340 --> 00:46:13.900
And we had magnificent students who came

00:46:13.900 --> 00:46:16.983
in that first tranche of people.

00:46:20.210 --> 00:46:22.850
I would rank the coeducation thing

00:46:22.850 --> 00:46:24.900
as the second most difficult,

00:46:24.900 --> 00:46:26.800
because we only had seven months,

00:46:26.800 --> 00:46:29.850
but there's no doubt that
having only six weeks

00:46:29.850 --> 00:46:34.090
to prepare for May Day
was the most difficult

00:46:34.090 --> 00:46:36.970
of the things I was involved with.

00:46:36.970 --> 00:46:40.090
But I will also say that
this guy learned more

00:46:40.090 --> 00:46:42.150
from those three events
than he ever learned

00:46:42.150 --> 00:46:43.480
in the rest of his life,

00:46:43.480 --> 00:46:45.550
and I'd learned so many different things

00:46:45.550 --> 00:46:48.853
about people, about human nature,

00:46:50.060 --> 00:46:53.227
I'd learn about minority events

00:46:53.227 --> 00:46:55.580
that never would have occurred to me

00:46:55.580 --> 00:46:59.320
if it hadn't been for those
students beating on our door,

00:46:59.320 --> 00:47:02.630
pushing us hard for financial
aid and those kinds of things.

00:47:02.630 --> 00:47:04.390
And the same with all of you.

00:47:04.390 --> 00:47:06.083
I learned an enormous amount.

00:47:08.079 --> 00:47:10.310
I mean, I think those kind of experiences

00:47:10.310 --> 00:47:12.326
are what really make your life,

00:47:12.326 --> 00:47:14.430
and they were early in
my life, so I was lucky.

00:47:14.430 --> 00:47:16.170
- So can you give us one example

00:47:16.170 --> 00:47:18.373
that is emblematic for that?

00:47:24.370 --> 00:47:25.203
- Yeah.

00:47:27.240 --> 00:47:28.910
Is Shirley Daniels here?

00:47:28.910 --> 00:47:30.060
- Yes.
- Where is she?

00:47:31.520 --> 00:47:34.523
Shirley Daniels.
(applause)

00:47:36.820 --> 00:47:41.820
Shirley and another minority
student, Euclid Irving,

00:47:43.970 --> 00:47:47.410
were assigned by the
Black Student Association

00:47:47.410 --> 00:47:48.717
to deal with me.

00:47:48.717 --> 00:47:49.550
(laughter)

00:47:52.100 --> 00:47:53.890
And I don't know, Shirley,
how many times ...

00:47:53.890 --> 00:47:58.210
I recall we met Friday at, I don't know,

00:47:58.210 --> 00:48:01.090
11 o'clock every morning
or something like that,

00:48:01.090 --> 00:48:03.577
and, you know, I kind of
thought, "You know students,

00:48:03.577 --> 00:48:06.377
"they won't show up after the
first couple of meetings."

00:48:07.276 --> 00:48:09.810
Well, Shirley and Euclid were
there every single Friday.

00:48:09.810 --> 00:48:11.903
And every single Friday, they pushed me.

00:48:13.710 --> 00:48:15.260
About the financial, we were talking

00:48:15.260 --> 00:48:17.350
primarily about financial aid.

00:48:17.350 --> 00:48:19.710
I learned things that I
never would have learned.

00:48:19.710 --> 00:48:22.530
I came from a family that
didn't have a lot of money.

00:48:22.530 --> 00:48:24.010
I was a scholarship student at Yale,

00:48:24.010 --> 00:48:25.210
but I was very lucky.

00:48:25.210 --> 00:48:27.400
I came from a family that had,

00:48:27.400 --> 00:48:30.570
but I didn't understand
the fact that, for example,

00:48:30.570 --> 00:48:33.203
that if a minority student came here,

00:48:34.710 --> 00:48:37.830
it was not only needing the financial aid,

00:48:37.830 --> 00:48:40.550
but it was the loss of his or her income

00:48:40.550 --> 00:48:44.850
to the family income that was
devastating for the family.

00:48:44.850 --> 00:48:45.811
Well now, this is the kind of thing

00:48:45.811 --> 00:48:46.644
I wouldn't have ever learned

00:48:46.644 --> 00:48:51.240
if it hadn't been for Shirley and Euclid

00:48:53.340 --> 00:48:57.770
coming after me, and I think
that was incredibly important

00:48:57.770 --> 00:49:01.330
in my education, to see issues

00:49:01.330 --> 00:49:04.583
that I didn't ever
encounter in my own life.

00:49:05.500 --> 00:49:07.560
- Let's open, this is fascinating, Sam.

00:49:07.560 --> 00:49:09.633
I could continue for quite a while,

00:49:10.740 --> 00:49:13.540
but I know people here would
like to ask some questions.

00:49:14.500 --> 00:49:17.321
- Are you gonna start to filter
them in case they're angry?

00:49:17.321 --> 00:49:18.580
(laughter)
- No.

00:49:18.580 --> 00:49:19.720
Because you sounded as if you were

00:49:19.720 --> 00:49:23.790
incredibly adept at hanging,
at handling that sort of thing.

00:49:23.790 --> 00:49:27.400
So, I know I ignored the
right side of the room today,

00:49:27.400 --> 00:49:31.030
so I wanna take questions
from wherever they come,

00:49:31.030 --> 00:49:34.363
but, yes, the lady in the red jacket.

00:49:39.720 --> 00:49:41.773
Are there microphone?
- Are there any microphones?

00:49:48.010 --> 00:49:48.843
Here we go.

00:49:51.031 --> 00:49:56.031
- [Barbara] I'm Barbara
Wagner, class of '73.

00:49:56.470 --> 00:50:00.860
We've heard a lot of talk
about Yale and Princeton

00:50:00.860 --> 00:50:02.860
and Harvard and the Ivy League.

00:50:02.860 --> 00:50:04.560
And I have a very good friend who went

00:50:04.560 --> 00:50:06.900
to the University of
Virginia the next year,

00:50:06.900 --> 00:50:08.211
the first co-educated class,

00:50:08.211 --> 00:50:11.470
which is virtually done under court order,

00:50:11.470 --> 00:50:14.090
and they didn't even bother
taking out the urinals

00:50:14.090 --> 00:50:15.040
because they didn't want ...

00:50:15.040 --> 00:50:16.840
No one wanted the women there.

00:50:16.840 --> 00:50:20.830
I'm curious whether Yale paid, you know,

00:50:20.830 --> 00:50:22.830
was the Ivy League oblivious to,

00:50:22.830 --> 00:50:23.910
and I have another friend who went

00:50:23.910 --> 00:50:25.460
to the University of North Carolina

00:50:25.460 --> 00:50:29.740
in the first freshman class
that admitted women in 1965.

00:50:29.740 --> 00:50:33.940
I'm curious if Yale was
following the trends

00:50:33.940 --> 00:50:36.250
among other previously all-male schools,

00:50:36.250 --> 00:50:39.870
or was it totally focused
on the Ivy League,

00:50:39.870 --> 00:50:44.610
and aware of not just the
trend of coeducational schools

00:50:44.610 --> 00:50:47.700
and faculty who taught
it, state universities,

00:50:47.700 --> 00:50:50.080
but other schools around the country

00:50:50.080 --> 00:50:51.913
that were starting to go coed?

00:50:54.849 --> 00:50:57.140
- I'm a little, I'm ashamed of this answer

00:50:57.140 --> 00:51:01.530
and I was ashamed this
morning, I guess it was,

00:51:01.530 --> 00:51:06.530
when the panel that had Nancy
Malkiel and company on it,

00:51:09.980 --> 00:51:13.154
some of whom are a little
critical in our planning.

00:51:13.154 --> 00:51:14.930
(laughter)

00:51:14.930 --> 00:51:18.060
We did not really know we were

00:51:18.060 --> 00:51:22.133
going to be coeducational
until November of 1968.

00:51:23.700 --> 00:51:25.024
The trustees, on the whole
were, we thought were favorable,

00:51:25.024 --> 00:51:30.024
but there were two or three who
felt so strongly against it,

00:51:31.130 --> 00:51:33.903
we weren't sure how the
board would actually vote.

00:51:34.890 --> 00:51:36.190
And from that moment on,

00:51:36.190 --> 00:51:40.270
it was like being in a
long-distance race, you know?

00:51:40.270 --> 00:51:41.670
You just had to start running.

00:51:41.670 --> 00:51:45.220
So, we did not do any studies or anything.

00:51:45.220 --> 00:51:48.200
The only thing we did
that was really smart,

00:51:48.200 --> 00:51:51.210
and I'm unable to remember her name,

00:51:51.210 --> 00:51:53.010
someone may here remember.

00:51:53.010 --> 00:51:55.564
There was a wonderful woman who was,

00:51:55.564 --> 00:52:00.043
have been president of
Barnard and president of ...

00:52:01.260 --> 00:52:03.873
Became vice chancellor of UCLA.

00:52:06.030 --> 00:52:07.970
And I'll, maybe, her name will come to me,

00:52:07.970 --> 00:52:10.720
but we did ask her to come and talk to us

00:52:10.720 --> 00:52:12.943
about coeducation and some of the,

00:52:14.020 --> 00:52:17.800
some of the ideas, and she
was wonderful and brilliant.

00:52:17.800 --> 00:52:21.340
But we didn't do studies
or anything like that.

00:52:21.340 --> 00:52:23.690
We just got our head down and figured out

00:52:23.690 --> 00:52:27.730
how we were gonna get
500 women into the place,

00:52:27.730 --> 00:52:29.780
where we're going to house them,

00:52:29.780 --> 00:52:31.239
how are we going to make
them safe as we could,

00:52:31.239 --> 00:52:33.870
and those issues.

00:52:33.870 --> 00:52:36.740
So, I'm embarrassed to answer
this question that way,

00:52:36.740 --> 00:52:38.143
but no, we did not.

00:52:39.420 --> 00:52:41.103
- I'll go here and then there.

00:52:42.356 --> 00:52:45.356
(audience laughing)

00:52:50.143 --> 00:52:50.976
- Here you go, right behind you.

00:52:50.976 --> 00:52:51.903
- No.
- Oh there, OK.

00:52:53.400 --> 00:52:55.850
- [Caroline] So my question, whoo.

00:52:55.850 --> 00:52:57.230
Oh, I'm Caroline Britelle.

00:52:57.230 --> 00:52:59.763
I'm from Jonathan Edward
College class of '71.

00:53:01.510 --> 00:53:03.310
I have a scrap book and there are actually

00:53:03.310 --> 00:53:05.300
two letters from Yale in that scrapbook.

00:53:05.300 --> 00:53:08.653
There's one that's in
March saying, you now have,

00:53:09.760 --> 00:53:12.030
we're now asking you to
send additional materials.

00:53:12.030 --> 00:53:15.400
And then there was dated
something April 19th,

00:53:15.400 --> 00:53:17.390
the actual acceptance latter.

00:53:17.390 --> 00:53:19.210
And so I was wondering
if you could explain

00:53:19.210 --> 00:53:21.640
a little bit about the
process that went on

00:53:21.640 --> 00:53:24.470
and also did you, were you overwhelmed

00:53:24.470 --> 00:53:25.810
by the number of applications?

00:53:25.810 --> 00:53:27.350
Were there more than you expected?

00:53:27.350 --> 00:53:28.583
Less than you expected?

00:53:29.740 --> 00:53:31.040
- Well, as to expect it,

00:53:31.040 --> 00:53:34.130
we had no idea how many
applicants would be.

00:53:34.130 --> 00:53:38.000
But remember that the decision was made

00:53:38.000 --> 00:53:39.833
in November of 1968.

00:53:43.680 --> 00:53:47.993
The admission of men for
that year had already begun,

00:53:49.250 --> 00:53:51.370
and so the admissions office,

00:53:51.370 --> 00:53:52.870
the undergraduate admissions office

00:53:52.870 --> 00:53:55.993
was already underway
admitting a class of men.

00:53:56.840 --> 00:54:00.000
So we made the decision,
Elga and I made the decision

00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:02.360
that we would let them do the paperwork.

00:54:02.360 --> 00:54:04.720
They had assembled all the documents,

00:54:04.720 --> 00:54:07.070
sent out the printed materials and so on.

00:54:07.070 --> 00:54:08.880
But that she and I would do the whole

00:54:08.880 --> 00:54:10.883
admissions process ourselves.

00:54:12.450 --> 00:54:17.450
So I'm not sure, but I think
we got 11,000 aplicants,

00:54:19.448 --> 00:54:21.740
but I'm not 100% sure of that figure,

00:54:21.740 --> 00:54:23.290
but I think that's what it was.

00:54:25.020 --> 00:54:30.020
So we knew that we
couldn't get our work done

00:54:30.510 --> 00:54:34.253
as fast as the mail team was getting,

00:54:35.870 --> 00:54:38.320
admission of men was getting done.

00:54:38.320 --> 00:54:42.800
So I think we send out materials,

00:54:42.800 --> 00:54:46.060
instead of what you would do
in a normal admissions process,

00:54:46.060 --> 00:54:47.660
if we saw something was massing,

00:54:47.660 --> 00:54:49.310
we immediately wrote to that person

00:54:49.310 --> 00:54:51.170
and said we didn't get
your teacher's report

00:54:51.170 --> 00:54:53.350
or we didn't get this
or we didn't get that.

00:54:53.350 --> 00:54:55.410
So it was a little bit
of sort of rolling along

00:54:55.410 --> 00:54:57.430
and we make a decision that this person

00:54:57.430 --> 00:55:00.400
was gonna get in and
we put that file aside.

00:55:00.400 --> 00:55:03.683
I have a vivid memory of a March night,

00:55:04.725 --> 00:55:08.190
it was a miserable night,
and I walked back to my home

00:55:08.190 --> 00:55:11.270
with one of those great big file boxes

00:55:11.270 --> 00:55:13.350
with nothing but admissions folders in it.

00:55:13.350 --> 00:55:15.050
I don't know how many hundred were in it.

00:55:15.050 --> 00:55:18.010
And I knew I had to read them
all before the next morning.

00:55:18.010 --> 00:55:20.913
And I looked down the file,

00:55:21.780 --> 00:55:23.610
and they all looked exactly the same.

00:55:23.610 --> 00:55:24.950
You could see the pink form

00:55:24.950 --> 00:55:26.500
and the blue form and the white form,

00:55:26.500 --> 00:55:30.260
but then there was in the
middle that was very thick.

00:55:30.260 --> 00:55:33.640
And I said to myself, that's the only one

00:55:33.640 --> 00:55:35.373
who's gonna be interesting.

00:55:35.373 --> 00:55:36.206
(audience laughing)

00:55:36.206 --> 00:55:38.480
And so I put it at the very end.

00:55:38.480 --> 00:55:40.220
Because I knew I had to stay awake,

00:55:40.220 --> 00:55:42.520
and that one would keep me awake.

00:55:42.520 --> 00:55:47.520
So it was a process, now,
in the, one of the panels,

00:55:47.910 --> 00:55:50.580
they talked about the things

00:55:50.580 --> 00:55:52.330
that Elga and I were thinking about

00:55:54.271 --> 00:55:56.150
and the famous remark I made

00:55:56.150 --> 00:55:58.210
that if you had four brothers, you got in.

00:55:58.210 --> 00:55:59.823
Well, that was absolutely true.

00:56:00.670 --> 00:56:03.147
And there was one woman
the other night who came

00:56:03.147 --> 00:56:04.510
and there you are back there,

00:56:04.510 --> 00:56:05.993
she had four brothers.

00:56:07.980 --> 00:56:11.180
If you had three bothers,
you were likely to get in,

00:56:11.180 --> 00:56:13.430
and if you had two, we
certainly looked hard.

00:56:14.390 --> 00:56:19.030
The second thing we look for
was women who had experiences

00:56:19.980 --> 00:56:22.040
that would toughen them up.

00:56:22.040 --> 00:56:25.740
And that might have been being a violinist

00:56:25.740 --> 00:56:29.860
who had achieved some
kind of national stature,

00:56:29.860 --> 00:56:32.860
it might have been an athlete

00:56:32.860 --> 00:56:35.960
who had achieved some kind of stature.

00:56:35.960 --> 00:56:38.910
There was another woman
who I talked with today

00:56:38.910 --> 00:56:42.770
who had lived for the previous two years

00:56:42.770 --> 00:56:45.500
before applying in other countries.

00:56:45.500 --> 00:56:50.080
So we of course had a
kind of academic cut off,

00:56:50.080 --> 00:56:52.960
but we were mostly looking for people

00:56:52.960 --> 00:56:57.960
'cause we knew that this was
gonna be a very difficult place

00:56:59.410 --> 00:57:01.700
for a lot of women to come to.

00:57:01.700 --> 00:57:04.660
And I think we avoid it,

00:57:04.660 --> 00:57:06.060
and I hope that we didn't avoid them all,

00:57:06.060 --> 00:57:09.963
but I think we avoided a
woman who was an only child.

00:57:11.604 --> 00:57:12.437
(audience laughing)

00:57:12.437 --> 00:57:14.103
Okay, apparently we did, okay.

00:57:15.450 --> 00:57:16.703
Maybe we didn't know.

00:57:17.930 --> 00:57:22.060
Anyway, we were reluctant to admit someone

00:57:22.060 --> 00:57:24.510
who we thought might find the hostility

00:57:24.510 --> 00:57:26.440
of the masculine campus

00:57:27.630 --> 00:57:30.130
something that they couldn't deal with.

00:57:30.130 --> 00:57:31.623
- [Krista] So, can I?

00:57:33.590 --> 00:57:38.590
Yeah, my name is Krista
Tran, class of '71.

00:57:38.680 --> 00:57:43.030
I'd like to ask you
about probably everyone's

00:57:43.030 --> 00:57:48.030
most heated choke point
was Yale's obligation

00:57:48.200 --> 00:57:50.500
to the nation to produce 1,000

00:57:50.500 --> 00:57:53.670
future male leaders of America,

00:57:53.670 --> 00:57:56.150
and many of us, if you ask,

00:57:56.150 --> 00:58:01.150
this would be the number one
thing we, was a controversy.

00:58:01.431 --> 00:58:02.850
And I would like to know what your

00:58:02.850 --> 00:58:07.850
conversations with him about that were.

00:58:08.140 --> 00:58:08.973
- Yeah.

00:58:10.345 --> 00:58:12.610
To be sure everyone knows the context,

00:58:12.610 --> 00:58:15.300
Kingman had said, and I don't remember

00:58:15.300 --> 00:58:16.800
exactly when he first said it,

00:58:18.250 --> 00:58:22.063
we were obligated to
educate 1,000 male leaders.

00:58:23.450 --> 00:58:25.520
Never has a greater mistake

00:58:25.520 --> 00:58:27.813
ever been made by a Yale president

00:58:27.813 --> 00:58:29.713
(audience laughing)
than saying that.

00:58:30.618 --> 00:58:34.983
And I guess we sorta knew
it pretty quickly on.

00:58:36.530 --> 00:58:41.170
It came in part from our
fear of what the alumni

00:58:41.170 --> 00:58:43.890
reaction would be to
the admission of women.

00:58:43.890 --> 00:58:48.023
Because we didn't have any,
talk about not making studies,

00:58:48.023 --> 00:58:49.240
we hadn't sort of polled the alumni

00:58:49.240 --> 00:58:51.750
or done anything to find
out how would they feel.

00:58:51.750 --> 00:58:53.410
We had the sense that the alumni

00:58:53.410 --> 00:58:56.710
would be very opposed to coeducation.

00:58:56.710 --> 00:58:58.490
That was wrong.

00:58:58.490 --> 00:59:00.160
As John Wilkinson said this morning,

00:59:00.160 --> 00:59:03.070
the minute a male alumnus woke up

00:59:03.070 --> 00:59:04.400
and realized he had a daughter,

00:59:04.400 --> 00:59:06.700
he all of a sudden thought
it was a good idea.

00:59:08.180 --> 00:59:12.330
But having said that,
Brewster had the problem

00:59:12.330 --> 00:59:13.743
of how to back off of it.

00:59:14.930 --> 00:59:16.750
This was combined with our,

00:59:16.750 --> 00:59:19.140
the way we were gonna deal with it,

00:59:19.140 --> 00:59:21.790
was we were gonna build two
new residential colleges

00:59:21.790 --> 00:59:24.143
on the corner of Grove and Whitney.

00:59:25.100 --> 00:59:28.390
And we had the money and
we had the plans drawn

00:59:28.390 --> 00:59:30.330
and we had the building,
everything torn down

00:59:30.330 --> 00:59:32.530
in the corner and then of
course the City of New Haven

00:59:32.530 --> 00:59:35.610
wouldn't give us the permit to build 'em.

00:59:35.610 --> 00:59:38.683
So that pulled out the
rug out from under there.

00:59:40.780 --> 00:59:44.940
I think, I think Kingman
knew he had made a mistake,

00:59:44.940 --> 00:59:48.950
but I think he felt having
made the public statement

00:59:48.950 --> 00:59:51.490
that he had to sorta back it up.

00:59:51.490 --> 00:59:56.190
But he gently backed off,
and I can't actually remember

00:59:57.050 --> 01:00:00.030
the exact numbers as the years go on,

01:00:00.030 --> 01:00:03.680
but I think he was willing
to back off of that

01:00:03.680 --> 01:00:04.513
but he didn't wanna have

01:00:04.513 --> 01:00:05.910
to make a public statement about it.

01:00:05.910 --> 01:00:09.780
- [Krista] Did you ever
tell him it was a mistake?

01:00:09.780 --> 01:00:11.802
- Everybody told him it was a mistake.

01:00:11.802 --> 01:00:13.402
(audience laughing)
No, I mean,

01:00:14.310 --> 01:00:16.200
that's too easy to say.

01:00:16.200 --> 01:00:19.300
When he said it, I knew
why he was saying it.

01:00:19.300 --> 01:00:21.130
We had come out of a meeting in New York

01:00:21.130 --> 01:00:25.320
with the Lux Et Veritas group
that were out for his scalp,

01:00:25.320 --> 01:00:28.723
and they told us if we admitted women,

01:00:28.723 --> 01:00:30.478
they were gonna come after us.

01:00:30.478 --> 01:00:35.230
And so we, I would actually have to say

01:00:35.230 --> 01:00:37.422
I went along with him when he made it.

01:00:37.422 --> 01:00:39.290
But it was a terrible mistake.

01:00:39.290 --> 01:00:42.810
- You said something in
review following up on that,

01:00:42.810 --> 01:00:45.030
in the Yale alumni magazine,

01:00:45.030 --> 01:00:48.130
new one on the stands or whatever,

01:00:48.130 --> 01:00:51.190
that, something about speaking of context

01:00:51.190 --> 01:00:56.190
that Kingman also was
looking at fellow Ivy leaders

01:00:57.380 --> 01:00:59.380
who were losing their jobs.
- Yes.

01:00:59.380 --> 01:01:01.047
- Okay, explain that a little.

01:01:02.930 --> 01:01:06.220
- In those period, and I can't
give you all the exact dates,

01:01:06.220 --> 01:01:10.200
Nate Pusey at Harvard,
Grayson Kirk at Columbia,

01:01:10.200 --> 01:01:11.823
Gaylord Harnwell at Penn,

01:01:14.785 --> 01:01:16.910
and one other, all lost their jobs

01:01:16.910 --> 01:01:21.580
because radical movements had resulted

01:01:21.580 --> 01:01:26.580
in terrible injury, fires, people died.

01:01:28.190 --> 01:01:30.580
And it was felt that these individuals

01:01:30.580 --> 01:01:32.920
were not up to the times and so on.

01:01:32.920 --> 01:01:35.360
And Kingman was constantly

01:01:35.360 --> 01:01:38.183
trying to wend his way between the two.

01:01:39.840 --> 01:01:41.770
Nancy Malkiel mentioned something today

01:01:41.770 --> 01:01:43.870
which is I think we're talking about,

01:01:43.870 --> 01:01:46.190
and that was she mentioned that Kingman

01:01:46.190 --> 01:01:48.633
had made a comment about the,

01:01:49.770 --> 01:01:51.480
his belief that a Black person

01:01:51.480 --> 01:01:53.903
could not get a fair trial
in the United States.

01:01:55.540 --> 01:02:00.540
And the second one was his
anti Vietnam War declaration.

01:02:03.940 --> 01:02:06.010
One of the things that
Kingman and I talked about

01:02:06.010 --> 01:02:07.990
and Charlie Taylor enormous amount

01:02:07.990 --> 01:02:12.910
was what is right for a
college, a university president

01:02:12.910 --> 01:02:17.160
to talk about that's of a
public, of a national stature,

01:02:17.160 --> 01:02:19.793
as opposed to a local university thing.

01:02:21.350 --> 01:02:26.350
So on the fair trial, Black
person getting a fair trial,

01:02:28.130 --> 01:02:30.780
we were going into the May Day business.

01:02:30.780 --> 01:02:33.940
A judge, the judge in
the Bobby Seale trial

01:02:34.830 --> 01:02:39.830
in preliminary hearings
had seen two Black men

01:02:40.060 --> 01:02:42.670
in the back of the room
reading a newspaper.

01:02:42.670 --> 01:02:44.680
And he had found them in contempt of court

01:02:44.680 --> 01:02:45.980
and put them in jail.

01:02:45.980 --> 01:02:48.870
And this just went like a spark.

01:02:48.870 --> 01:02:51.540
And the students at Yale were enraged,

01:02:51.540 --> 01:02:55.130
and the Black community
in New Haven was in rage.

01:02:55.130 --> 01:02:57.760
And Kingman as a lawyer was enraged

01:02:57.760 --> 01:02:59.993
to have seen a judge do that.

01:03:00.930 --> 01:03:05.930
So we spent 48 hours
writing that sentence.

01:03:07.380 --> 01:03:10.740
And then he put it in a talk
to the Yale College faculty

01:03:10.740 --> 01:03:13.540
and allowed the Yale news to
attend the faculty meeting.

01:03:14.600 --> 01:03:16.513
He wanted that statement out.

01:03:18.160 --> 01:03:21.520
In the Vietnam War, he eventually made

01:03:21.520 --> 01:03:25.000
a very strong statement
against the Vietnam War.

01:03:25.000 --> 01:03:27.300
A lot of people don't
know that one of Kingman's

01:03:27.300 --> 01:03:29.930
very closest friends was McGeorge Bundy,

01:03:29.930 --> 01:03:32.610
who at that time was the
National Security Advisor

01:03:32.610 --> 01:03:33.863
to Lyndon Johnson.

01:03:34.960 --> 01:03:37.843
Mac Bundy was one of the
architects of the Vietnam War.

01:03:39.020 --> 01:03:41.730
I remember two occasions
with Kingman and Mac.

01:03:41.730 --> 01:03:44.860
One in the Caribbean and one in New Haven,

01:03:44.860 --> 01:03:48.690
in which the shouting
match was really something

01:03:48.690 --> 01:03:50.590
as they fought over the issue.

01:03:50.590 --> 01:03:53.367
And I remember I said to Mac,

01:03:53.367 --> 01:03:55.000
"You should get out of that damn place."

01:03:55.000 --> 01:03:58.500
And he looked at me and he
said, "You're an American."

01:03:58.500 --> 01:04:00.050
This was really bitter stuff

01:04:00.050 --> 01:04:01.930
between people who are all friends.

01:04:01.930 --> 01:04:03.273
We all liked each other.

01:04:04.220 --> 01:04:09.220
But Kingman's belief was
that if the national issue

01:04:11.990 --> 01:04:15.760
affected the student body or the faculty,

01:04:15.760 --> 01:04:17.983
he had an obligation to speak out on it.

01:04:19.120 --> 01:04:23.810
And that was the place he drew,
the place he drew the line.

01:04:23.810 --> 01:04:26.200
- Which I think, I won't
ask Peter to comment,

01:04:26.200 --> 01:04:29.560
but President Rick Levent
(?) dealt with that,

01:04:29.560 --> 01:04:34.560
and it's, are you intellectual
leader in this country,

01:04:35.740 --> 01:04:39.290
but where they go, where is the line?

01:04:39.290 --> 01:04:41.930
Now there's someone over here in navy blue

01:04:41.930 --> 01:04:44.523
who's been dying to speak, go ahead.

01:04:45.816 --> 01:04:47.913
- This is--
- Oh no, wait.

01:04:47.913 --> 01:04:49.186
You need to stand up--
- You need a microphone.

01:04:49.186 --> 01:04:50.980
- And you need a microphone.

01:04:50.980 --> 01:04:52.150
- [Audience Member] This is very trivial

01:04:52.150 --> 01:04:54.350
and it goes back three questions.

01:04:54.350 --> 01:04:56.620
What was the role of, what was the role

01:04:56.620 --> 01:04:59.463
of Inky Clark in admitting women?

01:05:01.760 --> 01:05:05.300
- When Arthur Howe resigned
as dean of admissions,

01:05:05.300 --> 01:05:09.350
and Arthur Howe is an
under-recognized man in Yale history.

01:05:09.350 --> 01:05:10.970
He was the one who really believed

01:05:10.970 --> 01:05:13.507
in the admission of minority students

01:05:13.507 --> 01:05:14.410
and started the talk and he was the one

01:05:14.410 --> 01:05:16.840
who suggested to the New York Times

01:05:16.840 --> 01:05:19.830
that women ought to be admitted in 1956.

01:05:19.830 --> 01:05:21.440
And Whit Griswold had that wonderful,

01:05:21.440 --> 01:05:22.587
called the man and said,

01:05:22.587 --> 01:05:25.257
"Arthur, I agree with you
about admitting women,

01:05:25.257 --> 01:05:27.287
"but don't you ever dare say it again."

01:05:28.460 --> 01:05:30.723
- At least in public.
- In public, yeah.

01:05:31.870 --> 01:05:35.200
He resigned in 1963 to go do,

01:05:35.200 --> 01:05:37.140
head the American Field Service,

01:05:37.140 --> 01:05:41.120
and came and decided that that staff

01:05:41.120 --> 01:05:42.950
that was there was too old,

01:05:42.950 --> 01:05:46.750
and not a modern group
of admissions people.

01:05:46.750 --> 01:05:51.373
And so he chose Inky Clark to head it up.

01:05:53.660 --> 01:05:55.720
Clark was carrying out what Arthur Howe

01:05:55.720 --> 01:05:57.933
had started, in many ways.

01:05:59.630 --> 01:06:02.150
Inky Clark was great.

01:06:02.150 --> 01:06:05.320
He hired a magnificent
young group of people

01:06:05.320 --> 01:06:07.343
to come in and do this thing.

01:06:09.210 --> 01:06:10.910
He did what he was asked to do,

01:06:10.910 --> 01:06:13.113
which was to change the face of Yale.

01:06:14.040 --> 01:06:17.373
His great weakness was that
he had no sense of diplomacy.

01:06:18.220 --> 01:06:21.980
And he was abrupt and straightforward.

01:06:21.980 --> 01:06:23.900
What he said was right,
but he kinda said it

01:06:23.900 --> 01:06:26.743
in a way that didn't,
was a little bit angry.

01:06:28.170 --> 01:06:30.810
But he was very good because he brought

01:06:30.810 --> 01:06:33.730
the institution from an old fashioned way

01:06:33.730 --> 01:06:35.830
of looking at things to a modern way.

01:06:35.830 --> 01:06:38.640
And I think Inky felt he'd done his job.

01:06:38.640 --> 01:06:39.870
He didn't stay too long.

01:06:39.870 --> 01:06:40.970
I can't remember the number of years,

01:06:40.970 --> 01:06:43.010
maybe six years at the most.

01:06:43.010 --> 01:06:46.250
And then he became headmaster
of a school somewhere.

01:06:46.250 --> 01:06:48.810
But he was a great guy
and he was good at it

01:06:48.810 --> 01:06:52.190
but boy, he didn't, he never
went to diplomatic school.

01:06:52.190 --> 01:06:54.200
- No, and with the alums.

01:06:54.200 --> 01:06:56.243
Yes, congresswoman.

01:07:01.920 --> 01:07:04.119
- [Congresswoman] Presidents matter

01:07:04.119 --> 01:07:08.523
and I wanted to say, Sam, that
Kingman Brewster mattered.

01:07:10.880 --> 01:07:12.580
Thank you, Marta, very much,

01:07:12.580 --> 01:07:15.970
and to Marta and to Peter,

01:07:15.970 --> 01:07:20.680
it's not disrespectful, but
the tone that you've set

01:07:20.680 --> 01:07:22.943
in 2019 makes a difference.

01:07:23.900 --> 01:07:27.040
I just want to take this
brief moment to go back

01:07:27.040 --> 01:07:30.270
a point or two on the question

01:07:30.270 --> 01:07:35.110
of the African-American
students, Shirley and Euclid,

01:07:38.590 --> 01:07:43.590
and the diligence in
which the cultural groups

01:07:43.690 --> 01:07:47.320
made a difference and I can
pulse the push in that context.

01:07:47.320 --> 01:07:50.300
At the reception earlier
we were introduced

01:07:50.300 --> 01:07:52.280
to the cultural houses.

01:07:52.280 --> 01:07:57.240
Miraculously, under your
leadership in terms of that era,

01:07:57.240 --> 01:07:59.000
they're 50 years old too.

01:07:59.000 --> 01:08:00.840
No one would ever even imagine

01:08:02.080 --> 01:08:04.733
that they are 50 years
old Women Studies program.

01:08:05.857 --> 01:08:08.220
I think that was enormously innovative

01:08:08.220 --> 01:08:10.670
to have those programs at that time,

01:08:10.670 --> 01:08:14.420
even though we were
reaching for a diversity.

01:08:14.420 --> 01:08:17.670
So let me just focus on
the Afro-American house.

01:08:17.670 --> 01:08:21.220
As you well know, that
was not done without pain.

01:08:21.220 --> 01:08:24.580
You made the point that
they were brilliant men

01:08:24.580 --> 01:08:28.270
initially that came, scholars themselves,

01:08:28.270 --> 01:08:30.600
that pushed the university

01:08:30.600 --> 01:08:34.050
for the vitality and the importance

01:08:34.050 --> 01:08:36.390
of understanding African-American history.

01:08:36.390 --> 01:08:39.090
I love Yale for the fact
that I think history

01:08:39.090 --> 01:08:41.483
was really a theme that
ran throughout studies.

01:08:42.360 --> 01:08:46.290
Dr. Louis Gates is a
product of Yale University.

01:08:46.290 --> 01:08:48.050
We should never, he happens
to be a man, forgive me,

01:08:48.050 --> 01:08:53.050
but he was very respectful of
women students at the time.

01:08:54.020 --> 01:08:55.980
But the point is is that we should never

01:08:55.980 --> 01:08:59.130
shy away from being more
superior than Princeton

01:08:59.130 --> 01:09:02.010
on this experiment. (chuckles)

01:09:02.010 --> 01:09:04.040
Anybody went to grad school, that's okay.

01:09:04.040 --> 01:09:07.380
We should never shy away
for what we went through.

01:09:07.380 --> 01:09:09.650
And I'll just threw a personal anecdote,

01:09:09.650 --> 01:09:10.980
then I will just post a question,

01:09:10.980 --> 01:09:12.660
so I'll move quickly as possible.

01:09:12.660 --> 01:09:14.510
There was a question earlier today

01:09:14.510 --> 01:09:19.510
about how you compare 1969 in
terms of change to now 2019.

01:09:21.730 --> 01:09:24.790
So forgive me, my classmates,

01:09:24.790 --> 01:09:27.283
this happens to also
be the year of return.

01:09:28.240 --> 01:09:32.890
This is 400 years for
enslaved Africans who arrived

01:09:32.890 --> 01:09:36.310
in New England in 1619.

01:09:36.310 --> 01:09:38.930
What an interesting time
to come and do all these

01:09:38.930 --> 01:09:41.350
historical things at that time.

01:09:41.350 --> 01:09:43.930
But we became the mother
of African-American

01:09:43.930 --> 01:09:46.700
Studies programs across America.

01:09:46.700 --> 01:09:48.100
This is, we're not celebrating that now,

01:09:48.100 --> 01:09:50.020
but I'm just trying to put together

01:09:50.020 --> 01:09:52.170
all the historic, I think moments, Sam,

01:09:52.170 --> 01:09:54.403
that we were going through if I'm correct.

01:09:55.460 --> 01:10:00.460
And just this year, I took
up something that people

01:10:02.410 --> 01:10:07.410
would initially respond to as
what is a member of congress

01:10:07.440 --> 01:10:10.480
engaged in this kind of debate,

01:10:10.480 --> 01:10:14.050
and that is of course the
H.R.40 is a commission

01:10:14.050 --> 01:10:17.000
to study reparations proposals.

01:10:17.000 --> 01:10:20.920
And my goal was to reflect
on where I came from,

01:10:20.920 --> 01:10:25.723
but also Yale's pioneering
spirit about history.

01:10:26.830 --> 01:10:31.830
And so I view this
commission as an attempt,

01:10:33.150 --> 01:10:34.710
it hasn't been passed yet,

01:10:34.710 --> 01:10:36.430
but it's going further
than it's ever gone,

01:10:36.430 --> 01:10:38.930
leadership of the house,
and members of Congress

01:10:38.930 --> 01:10:41.190
from all over the nation co-sponsoring it,

01:10:41.190 --> 01:10:45.110
but the point is is that it's
about racial reconciliation

01:10:45.110 --> 01:10:47.080
and then ultimately what do you do

01:10:47.080 --> 01:10:48.880
in that period of history.

01:10:48.880 --> 01:10:53.350
My question for you as
you laid the groundwork

01:10:53.350 --> 01:10:57.890
for many of these folks,
Blassingame, if you remember,

01:10:57.890 --> 01:10:59.010
you brought him--
- John Blassingame.

01:10:59.010 --> 01:11:01.005
- [Congresswoman] You brought him here.

01:11:01.005 --> 01:11:02.960
He was considered a genius

01:11:02.960 --> 01:11:05.400
in terms of building that departments,

01:11:05.400 --> 01:11:07.730
boom, that surely highlighted.

01:11:07.730 --> 01:11:11.390
What do you view mixing women with that?

01:11:11.390 --> 01:11:13.870
How do you view that as a significant

01:11:13.870 --> 01:11:16.682
part of the leadership,

01:11:16.682 --> 01:11:20.317
this whole cultural revolution
of acknowledging Asians,

01:11:20.317 --> 01:11:23.743
Hispanics, Latinos,
African-Americans women,

01:11:27.176 --> 01:11:30.040
all groups that adhere
to for been left out

01:11:30.040 --> 01:11:32.820
and we unseeingly in that period of time

01:11:32.820 --> 01:11:35.181
were acknowledging all of them.

01:11:35.181 --> 01:11:38.180
I hope I left everybody, got everybody in,

01:11:38.180 --> 01:11:39.903
the Women Studies program.

01:11:40.896 --> 01:11:43.022
Did you all talk about that significance?

01:11:43.022 --> 01:11:44.763
Did they Yale Corporation board?

01:11:44.763 --> 01:11:46.593
Did they, were they impressed?

01:11:46.593 --> 01:11:48.393
Did they know what you did? (laughs)

01:11:49.885 --> 01:11:51.635
- Maybe not which was all for good.

01:11:52.470 --> 01:11:53.840
- [Congresswoman] And I
sit down on this note.

01:11:53.840 --> 01:11:58.473
Do they know that you kept
New Haven from imploding

01:11:59.440 --> 01:12:02.190
in May of '79 when Kingman shut down

01:12:02.190 --> 01:12:05.886
the university and said,
"Everybody, go out of class."

01:12:05.886 --> 01:12:08.170
I remember when, that was not in '69,

01:12:08.170 --> 01:12:11.920
it was '70, May Day,
'70, the spring of '70,

01:12:11.920 --> 01:12:13.610
when they led us out, as they said,

01:12:13.610 --> 01:12:16.510
'cause I remember in July
sort of digging myself in

01:12:16.510 --> 01:12:20.060
to get those papers in that
we didn't get in at that time.

01:12:20.060 --> 01:12:23.410
But I mean, all of that
was what women hear,

01:12:23.410 --> 01:12:25.400
and I wanna say to the women who are here,

01:12:25.400 --> 01:12:28.550
you are part of keeping the lid on.

01:12:28.550 --> 01:12:30.450
You really were.

01:12:30.450 --> 01:12:34.320
I think you were, most of
you were involved in this,

01:12:34.320 --> 01:12:37.100
and you kept the lid
on while we were really

01:12:37.100 --> 01:12:40.260
in one of the most explosive
historical moments,

01:12:40.260 --> 01:12:42.810
and hysterical moments of history.

01:12:42.810 --> 01:12:44.630
And we had all these different groups

01:12:44.630 --> 01:12:46.910
because the African-American
students were hold up

01:12:46.910 --> 01:12:50.060
at one of the secret society.

01:12:50.060 --> 01:12:51.480
Somebody else was somewhere else.

01:12:51.480 --> 01:12:53.350
Other people were hanging out in commons.

01:12:53.350 --> 01:12:57.420
We all had our spaces,
but we all seem to know

01:12:57.420 --> 01:13:01.763
that the administration
respected our dignity.

01:13:02.930 --> 01:13:06.010
And were going to stand
with us on that dignity.

01:13:06.010 --> 01:13:08.530
Otherwise, this place could have,

01:13:08.530 --> 01:13:11.300
I won't finish the
sentence, but, I yield back,

01:13:11.300 --> 01:13:12.722
as they say on the floor of the house.

01:13:12.722 --> 01:13:13.640
- I yield back.

01:13:13.640 --> 01:13:16.081
- [Congresswoman] Do they
know the significance of it?

01:13:16.081 --> 01:13:17.570
I guess that's my question.

01:13:17.570 --> 01:13:19.620
- Let me try and answer
couple of questions

01:13:19.620 --> 01:13:21.133
that are so embedded there.

01:13:22.075 --> 01:13:25.461
First of all, the Yale
trustees of Yale Corporation

01:13:25.461 --> 01:13:28.343
at that time was incredibly supportive.

01:13:29.950 --> 01:13:33.700
Some of you will remember
the meetings in the rink,

01:13:33.700 --> 01:13:38.340
7,000 people in the
rink to argue over ROTC

01:13:38.340 --> 01:13:41.090
or whatever it was we were arguing over.

01:13:41.090 --> 01:13:45.560
And when we had one of those meetings,

01:13:45.560 --> 01:13:47.400
we call the members of the corporation,

01:13:47.400 --> 01:13:50.310
and I would say of the 16 members,

01:13:50.310 --> 01:13:54.160
aside from the president, 14
were there for every event

01:13:54.160 --> 01:13:57.880
and sat in the rink and participated.

01:13:57.880 --> 01:14:00.753
They didn't speak, I don't
think, but they were there.

01:14:01.872 --> 01:14:02.705
So they were very supportive.

01:14:02.705 --> 01:14:07.620
We also had three, four,
great Afro-American trustees

01:14:10.080 --> 01:14:12.740
at the time, three were women,

01:14:12.740 --> 01:14:14.553
Marian Wright Edelman,

01:14:17.220 --> 01:14:19.863
Eleanor Holmes Norton,
and Barbara Preiskel.

01:14:21.411 --> 01:14:22.540
And then Judge Leon Higginbotham

01:14:22.540 --> 01:14:25.223
of the Federal Court was also.

01:14:26.286 --> 01:14:29.320
And those people gave us
guidance and understanding

01:14:29.320 --> 01:14:33.210
that we might never otherwise have had.

01:14:33.210 --> 01:14:35.690
Secondly, about the

01:14:38.666 --> 01:14:39.499
cultural houses.

01:14:40.420 --> 01:14:43.890
Some, I don't remember
how we learned it, but

01:14:45.420 --> 01:14:49.823
we knew that no matter how many initial,

01:14:50.690 --> 01:14:52.643
let's say Black students we admitted,

01:14:54.660 --> 01:14:59.660
they had to have a place
of, a sense of safety

01:14:59.961 --> 01:15:01.873
and getting together and so on.

01:15:02.910 --> 01:15:06.580
And so the idea of the cultural house

01:15:06.580 --> 01:15:10.920
seem to us to be almost
the number one thing

01:15:10.920 --> 01:15:12.173
that ought to be done.

01:15:14.840 --> 01:15:19.840
With regard to the
Afro-American studies major,

01:15:20.637 --> 01:15:23.150
the great heroes of this
were a Black student

01:15:23.150 --> 01:15:27.643
named Robbie Robinson and
Charles Taylor the provost.

01:15:28.911 --> 01:15:31.560
And Charlie who was provost and therefore

01:15:31.560 --> 01:15:33.864
he had a lot of things to do,

01:15:33.864 --> 01:15:38.864
undertook to personally
develop with Robbie,

01:15:39.300 --> 01:15:41.770
who was a senior and
there were other students

01:15:41.770 --> 01:15:44.544
on the committee, I've
forgotten all their names,

01:15:44.544 --> 01:15:47.570
but to undertake the
development of that major

01:15:47.570 --> 01:15:52.570
with the faculty and I
think everybody understood

01:15:54.500 --> 01:15:57.410
that in addition to needing a place

01:15:57.410 --> 01:16:01.870
where a small group of people
could feel comfortable,

01:16:01.870 --> 01:16:04.440
you needed an intellectual place

01:16:05.316 --> 01:16:07.193
in which they could also feel comfortable.

01:16:08.202 --> 01:16:11.240
And so the development of the
Afro-American studies major

01:16:11.240 --> 01:16:13.913
was to us of the highest priority.

01:16:15.380 --> 01:16:16.313
- Fascinating.

01:16:18.200 --> 01:16:20.513
Questions, yes, lady in the blue?

01:16:24.490 --> 01:16:25.570
- [Judith] We've been talking a lot

01:16:25.570 --> 01:16:30.409
about the arrival of women in 1969.

01:16:30.409 --> 01:16:31.677
- I'm sorry, the what?

01:16:31.677 --> 01:16:32.510
- [Judith] Sorry, Judith Fekyoni,

01:16:32.510 --> 01:16:35.430
1973, and Berkeley College.

01:16:35.430 --> 01:16:38.150
We've been talking a lot
about the arrival of women

01:16:38.150 --> 01:16:41.839
at Yale and I was
wondering if you could talk

01:16:41.839 --> 01:16:44.730
a little bit about the first
years of women on campus

01:16:44.730 --> 01:16:49.730
as part of Yale College
and particularly the belief

01:16:51.040 --> 01:16:54.574
that I had always had
but maybe it's not true,

01:16:54.574 --> 01:16:58.610
maybe it's imaginary, that
we were called the experiment

01:16:58.610 --> 01:17:02.133
in coeducation for the
entire time that I was here.

01:17:04.790 --> 01:17:06.140
- Is Ann here, Ann Perkins?

01:17:08.592 --> 01:17:09.910
Ann's, I guess she's not here.

01:17:09.910 --> 01:17:12.530
If you have not read Ann's book,

01:17:12.530 --> 01:17:14.973
you absolutely must read it.

01:17:15.847 --> 01:17:18.074
She has written a PhD dissertation

01:17:18.074 --> 01:17:21.220
which is transformed into
a very good history book,

01:17:21.220 --> 01:17:26.210
which documents the first two
years of coeducation at Yale.

01:17:26.210 --> 01:17:30.183
And I can't make it any better.

01:17:31.160 --> 01:17:33.500
I will say about it a couple of things.

01:17:33.500 --> 01:17:34.340
One is that

01:17:38.408 --> 01:17:42.036
I think without Elga, we
would've had a really tough time

01:17:42.036 --> 01:17:44.902
because I think she was
our spiritual leader

01:17:44.902 --> 01:17:47.973
as well as our administrative leader.

01:17:49.290 --> 01:17:52.860
And I think she was the one
who kept the fires burning

01:17:52.860 --> 01:17:56.040
in those first two years and kept the fire

01:17:56.040 --> 01:18:00.123
underneath our behinds
which was important.

01:18:02.650 --> 01:18:03.900
What was the second part?

01:18:06.421 --> 01:18:08.466
- Experiment.
- Oh, the experiment.

01:18:08.466 --> 01:18:10.185
Yeah, I don't know where that came from.

01:18:10.185 --> 01:18:11.640
It was not, we were not gonna back off,

01:18:11.640 --> 01:18:13.700
so there was never an intention at this

01:18:13.700 --> 01:18:17.476
where we try it out and see if
it worked and then deciding.

01:18:17.476 --> 01:18:19.340
I don't know where that came from

01:18:19.340 --> 01:18:22.833
except I think it may have come as a,

01:18:23.960 --> 01:18:27.150
from someone who was unhappy
that we weren't admitting

01:18:27.150 --> 01:18:29.970
more women and therefore use this

01:18:29.970 --> 01:18:32.743
as kind of a derogatory comment.

01:18:33.850 --> 01:18:36.580
But there was never any
intention of backing off

01:18:36.580 --> 01:18:37.680
and going back at all.

01:18:39.840 --> 01:18:42.330
- Is someone raising
their hand way back there?

01:18:42.330 --> 01:18:43.163
Yes.

01:18:48.020 --> 01:18:49.230
- [Bliswilliams] I'm curious,

01:18:49.230 --> 01:18:52.270
Bliswilliams Brown, Davenport '71.

01:18:52.270 --> 01:18:55.130
I'm curious whether the administration

01:18:55.130 --> 01:18:58.992
ever did anything to prepare
the men for our arrival?

01:18:58.992 --> 01:19:00.892
(audience laughing)
- Great question.

01:19:04.220 --> 01:19:06.830
- I'm gonna take the liberty of,

01:19:06.830 --> 01:19:09.140
before I answer your question.

01:19:09.140 --> 01:19:12.740
Somebody today in the afternoon seminar

01:19:12.740 --> 01:19:14.343
said something that I have,

01:19:15.210 --> 01:19:17.840
think is incredibly important.

01:19:17.840 --> 01:19:21.420
We need to teach men about themselves.

01:19:21.420 --> 01:19:26.420
We need a mandatory course for Yale males.

01:19:27.100 --> 01:19:30.930
Because the masculinity
of Yale is not yet gone.

01:19:30.930 --> 01:19:33.460
It's still very much a
part of the institution.

01:19:33.460 --> 01:19:36.410
Now, over to your question.

01:19:36.410 --> 01:19:39.880
Absolutely nothing.
(audience laughing)

01:19:39.880 --> 01:19:42.350
And I mean it, nothing was done.

01:19:42.350 --> 01:19:45.800
I'm not sure but what,
the word groups of men

01:19:45.800 --> 01:19:50.300
who were, who wanted to be
hospitable, and they, I think,

01:19:50.300 --> 01:19:52.740
an individual colleges
and individual groups,

01:19:52.740 --> 01:19:54.790
there were things that happened where men

01:19:55.720 --> 01:19:59.634
tried to be welcoming and
friendly and stuff like that.

01:19:59.634 --> 01:20:02.530
There is some disagreement, say,

01:20:02.530 --> 01:20:04.150
between John Wilkinson and myself.

01:20:04.150 --> 01:20:05.960
I think there were more undergraduate men

01:20:05.960 --> 01:20:09.520
who are against coeducation than he does.

01:20:09.520 --> 01:20:11.810
I think there were a fair number,

01:20:11.810 --> 01:20:15.693
I would think maybe 50% of the men who,

01:20:15.693 --> 01:20:18.530
it wasn't like they didn't
like women or something,

01:20:18.530 --> 01:20:22.000
it was just that they
thought Yale should be male.

01:20:22.000 --> 01:20:26.763
So I, but we didn't do
anything to educate the men.

01:20:28.707 --> 01:20:29.540
- Right there.

01:20:31.722 --> 01:20:35.143
Let's get the microphone.

01:20:37.670 --> 01:20:39.267
It's coming your way.
- Right behind you.

01:20:41.650 --> 01:20:46.190
- [Glen] Speaking, Glen
Murphy, Calhoon '71.

01:20:46.190 --> 01:20:50.120
Speaking as a male who was at Yale

01:20:50.120 --> 01:20:53.500
during both before and
after the coeducation

01:20:53.500 --> 01:20:57.450
I would remind you that coed week

01:20:57.450 --> 01:21:02.450
was instigated by the male
undergraduate population.

01:21:02.655 --> 01:21:05.110
- Avi Soyfer.
- Avi Soyfer.

01:21:05.110 --> 01:21:09.290
And we managed to accommodate 750 women

01:21:10.170 --> 01:21:13.150
without the world spinning off its axis.

01:21:13.150 --> 01:21:15.290
So I think it's fair to say

01:21:15.290 --> 01:21:19.360
that the male undergraduate
population, not in its entirety,

01:21:19.360 --> 01:21:24.070
but was out ahead of you as far
as coeducation was concerned

01:21:24.070 --> 01:21:27.190
and didn't need any
institutional preparation

01:21:27.190 --> 01:21:28.090
to make it happen.

01:21:29.090 --> 01:21:33.312
- Let me, I'm gonna, I'm gonna
pick a fight with you, okay?

01:21:33.312 --> 01:21:35.580
- Uh-oh.
- I don't think that's

01:21:35.580 --> 01:21:37.270
what the question she was talking about.

01:21:37.270 --> 01:21:38.110
You're absolutely right.

01:21:38.110 --> 01:21:40.590
Avi Soyfer did a fabulous job

01:21:40.590 --> 01:21:42.550
of putting together coeducation week

01:21:42.550 --> 01:21:46.290
and like four or 500 women came
to Yale and spent the week,

01:21:46.290 --> 01:21:49.370
went to classes, lived in
the dorms, and everything.

01:21:49.370 --> 01:21:52.570
But I think the question
is a different question.

01:21:52.570 --> 01:21:57.570
And that is that this institution,

01:21:58.890 --> 01:22:03.787
since 1701 until 1969, was all male,

01:22:08.300 --> 01:22:12.410
and it developed an atmosphere and a ethos

01:22:12.410 --> 01:22:15.700
which is embedded in every
wall and every ceiling

01:22:15.700 --> 01:22:18.870
and every floor of the institution.

01:22:18.870 --> 01:22:22.810
Everyone says, and I'm
a fan of Morris today,

01:22:22.810 --> 01:22:25.390
but everyone says Morris
is, they take women in,

01:22:25.390 --> 01:22:27.320
they have all these
nice parties and so on.

01:22:27.320 --> 01:22:29.900
You just go into the first
floor and find me a woman,

01:22:29.900 --> 01:22:31.800
a picture of a woman.

01:22:31.800 --> 01:22:34.630
There's no picture of any
woman on the first floor.

01:22:34.630 --> 01:22:35.660
They're on the stairways,

01:22:35.660 --> 01:22:38.160
they're up a stairs, they're hidden.

01:22:38.160 --> 01:22:40.860
Now I don't wanna pick on Morris.

01:22:40.860 --> 01:22:45.860
But I firmly believe,

01:22:47.320 --> 01:22:49.010
because I got so passionate

01:22:49.010 --> 01:22:50.490
about admitting women to Yale,

01:22:50.490 --> 01:22:52.900
I have spent my time
thinking about this subject.

01:22:52.900 --> 01:22:56.700
I have five or six undergraduate
advisees every year,

01:22:56.700 --> 01:22:58.760
I got about 20 in college now,

01:22:58.760 --> 01:23:01.020
half of them roughly are women.

01:23:01.020 --> 01:23:03.450
They tell me that they feel that Yale

01:23:03.450 --> 01:23:06.363
is still a masculine institution.

01:23:07.410 --> 01:23:09.896
Now they're not scared,
they're not afraid,

01:23:09.896 --> 01:23:12.410
they're going about their business.

01:23:12.410 --> 01:23:17.060
But I think men have to
learn about masculinity.

01:23:17.060 --> 01:23:18.893
Here we have Women Studies.

01:23:19.800 --> 01:23:22.420
We don't have Men Studies.

01:23:22.420 --> 01:23:24.610
You laugh, but listen.

01:23:24.610 --> 01:23:28.930
Men are not so good at understanding

01:23:28.930 --> 01:23:30.813
when they get together as a group,

01:23:31.680 --> 01:23:35.358
how at a place like Yale,
how offensive they can be.

01:23:35.358 --> 01:23:36.880
(audience laughing)

01:23:36.880 --> 01:23:40.490
So I know what you're talking about.

01:23:40.490 --> 01:23:45.490
And I agree that that coed
week was a great thing.

01:23:45.630 --> 01:23:49.280
But I think if, I hate to tell

01:23:49.280 --> 01:23:51.290
the president what to think about it.

01:23:51.290 --> 01:23:56.040
I do believe that if we wanna
be really coeducational,

01:23:56.040 --> 01:23:59.670
one of the things we have
to do is to teach men

01:23:59.670 --> 01:24:03.290
to understand what being masculine is

01:24:03.290 --> 01:24:05.820
and how it affects the other sex.

01:24:05.820 --> 01:24:09.070
Ending that.
(audience applauding)

01:24:09.070 --> 01:24:09.903
- Very interesting.

01:24:09.903 --> 01:24:11.910
I think, am I getting the signal?

01:24:11.910 --> 01:24:13.210
I think we have time.

01:24:13.210 --> 01:24:14.283
Yes, right here.

01:24:15.370 --> 01:24:18.245
- Hi, I'm Jen Castello.
- Don't get mad at me.

01:24:18.245 --> 01:24:22.083
- [Jen] Brandford, class of 1972.

01:24:23.020 --> 01:24:26.900
Like many of you, I'm sure, I
have been in alumni school's

01:24:26.900 --> 01:24:29.600
volunteer interviewer for many years,

01:24:29.600 --> 01:24:33.170
and I've been told by high
school students applying

01:24:33.170 --> 01:24:37.640
that Yale is well known
now as the gay-friendly Ivy

01:24:38.530 --> 01:24:40.800
when people were talking
about the cultural houses,

01:24:40.800 --> 01:24:43.810
it brought back memories
when I was here as a student,

01:24:43.810 --> 01:24:46.670
and volunteered for Dwight Hall.

01:24:46.670 --> 01:24:50.100
And there was a homosexuality
discussion group

01:24:50.100 --> 01:24:54.393
that gradually changed its
name openly to Gays at Yale.

01:24:55.590 --> 01:24:59.000
When we talk about bringing
women to a men's school,

01:24:59.000 --> 01:25:01.020
I remember there was some discussion

01:25:01.020 --> 01:25:02.460
about this was a good thing

01:25:02.460 --> 01:25:03.590
because it would make sure

01:25:03.590 --> 01:25:07.580
that there were heterosexual
manly men at Yale.

01:25:07.580 --> 01:25:10.430
And having close friends who
were gay men at the time,

01:25:10.430 --> 01:25:12.450
although I didn't have
a close women friend

01:25:12.450 --> 01:25:16.413
who identified as gay or
lesbian, I'm sure there are,

01:25:17.600 --> 01:25:20.373
it was still sort of
not a manly thing to do.

01:25:21.300 --> 01:25:24.390
The other thing is that I
won an award as a junior

01:25:24.390 --> 01:25:29.390
and the kelt for the qualities
that the award was named for

01:25:30.110 --> 01:25:32.144
and one of them was manliness.

01:25:32.144 --> 01:25:33.130
(audience laughing)

01:25:33.130 --> 01:25:36.780
And I used this a lot in
law school applications,

01:25:36.780 --> 01:25:39.930
that like, okay, not
only was I good student,

01:25:39.930 --> 01:25:41.223
but I was manly.

01:25:42.770 --> 01:25:45.130
It's always raised this question for me,

01:25:45.130 --> 01:25:47.770
was we know Yale is the mother of men

01:25:47.770 --> 01:25:50.610
and you'd swim naked in the pool

01:25:50.610 --> 01:25:53.650
and you'd lunch with the
other guys at Morris,

01:25:53.650 --> 01:25:56.630
and there was a reinforcement
of a particular kind

01:25:56.630 --> 01:26:00.610
of masculinity, but it was a
masculinity that presumably

01:26:00.610 --> 01:26:05.610
was quite fearful of being
perceived as feminine in any way.

01:26:06.580 --> 01:26:11.580
So I'm wondering now at what
point the change in Yale

01:26:14.180 --> 01:26:17.950
became such that it is
perceived as a friendly Ivy

01:26:17.950 --> 01:26:22.900
for gay, lesbian, queer,
binary, transgender,

01:26:22.900 --> 01:26:25.040
the whole new area of gender.

01:26:25.040 --> 01:26:27.720
Back when we were going to
Yale, it was only man or woman,

01:26:27.720 --> 01:26:30.960
but now there's gender
and gender identity.

01:26:30.960 --> 01:26:33.390
And if we're talking about how

01:26:33.390 --> 01:26:36.830
you identify yourself
as a person, masculine,

01:26:38.520 --> 01:26:40.720
still masculine environment like this,

01:26:40.720 --> 01:26:42.740
I think that'd be a really interesting

01:26:42.740 --> 01:26:44.863
area to discuss and to explore.

01:26:45.700 --> 01:26:49.470
Going back in time to
1969, did this come up

01:26:49.470 --> 01:26:51.860
in discussion that we
wanted the kind of men

01:26:51.860 --> 01:26:56.040
we wanted Yale to admit were
manly, heterosexual men,

01:26:56.040 --> 01:26:58.810
and we would bring in women
to make that possible?

01:26:58.810 --> 01:27:00.970
Or did nobody even talk about it?

01:27:00.970 --> 01:27:04.810
- No, in 1969, that subject
wouldn't have come up

01:27:04.810 --> 01:27:08.903
because those kinds of subjects
didn't come up ever in 1969.

01:27:10.600 --> 01:27:13.500
But I will say this, and
I'm not, I'm retired,

01:27:13.500 --> 01:27:14.900
I'm not an administrator now.

01:27:14.900 --> 01:27:16.960
But I will say this based on

01:27:16.960 --> 01:27:20.330
my advisees and other students I know.

01:27:20.330 --> 01:27:24.360
I marvel at how different
the world is today

01:27:24.360 --> 01:27:29.360
and the view of undergraduates
today about sexual issues.

01:27:29.680 --> 01:27:32.410
It is, it's a totally different world

01:27:32.410 --> 01:27:35.570
than the world I was brought
up in and maybe some of you,

01:27:35.570 --> 01:27:39.130
I don't know, I'm a
lot older than you are.

01:27:39.130 --> 01:27:43.120
But there's no question that
these, all these issues,

01:27:43.120 --> 01:27:45.470
I don't know whether
Yale's more gay-friendly

01:27:45.470 --> 01:27:47.700
or any other friendly than
any other institution.

01:27:47.700 --> 01:27:49.710
I just don't know whether
there's statistics

01:27:49.710 --> 01:27:51.800
that would bare that out or not.

01:27:51.800 --> 01:27:56.110
But I do know that the
issue of sexual identity

01:27:56.110 --> 01:27:59.150
or sexual orientation
or something like that

01:27:59.150 --> 01:28:02.050
is really a kind of not high

01:28:02.050 --> 01:28:05.600
on people's agenda in meeting each other.

01:28:05.600 --> 01:28:08.610
I mean, they may be
interested in the subject

01:28:08.610 --> 01:28:11.150
of sexual identity,
and wanna talk about it

01:28:11.150 --> 01:28:14.043
in a class or in a discussion group.

01:28:15.070 --> 01:28:17.180
But there are students at Yale

01:28:17.180 --> 01:28:21.960
who are friends of students who
have other sexual identities

01:28:21.960 --> 01:28:24.260
than they do and they
just all get along fine.

01:28:24.260 --> 01:28:28.700
So to me, that's an
issue that us oldtimers

01:28:28.700 --> 01:28:30.580
have to learn to deal with.

01:28:33.250 --> 01:28:35.570
- This will be the last question,

01:28:35.570 --> 01:28:38.763
and it's the lady also in blue, gray.

01:28:39.770 --> 01:28:40.603
Yes, you.

01:28:44.072 --> 01:28:45.144
(audience laughing)

01:28:45.144 --> 01:28:46.144
- Thank you.

01:28:47.030 --> 01:28:50.850
Hi, I'm Jena Weiss, class of '73.

01:28:50.850 --> 01:28:54.730
Earlier, you said you had a
disagreement with Jay Gitland

01:28:54.730 --> 01:28:58.180
about when the old Yale
became the new Yale.

01:28:58.180 --> 01:29:00.270
And you gave us your answer.

01:29:00.270 --> 01:29:04.163
I'm curious about what his answer was, is.

01:29:07.240 --> 01:29:10.053
- I wish Jay were here
and could defend himself.

01:29:14.692 --> 01:29:17.720
I think Jay is undecided.

01:29:17.720 --> 01:29:21.890
I think he thinks I'm wrong,
but I don't think he knows

01:29:21.890 --> 01:29:25.040
where he actually thinks the
old Yale became the new Yale.

01:29:25.040 --> 01:29:28.070
And I'm not sure actually
whether he thinks

01:29:28.070 --> 01:29:30.200
it has become the new Yale.

01:29:30.200 --> 01:29:32.830
So he may be, as an historian,

01:29:32.830 --> 01:29:36.950
he may see this more on
a kind of gradual level

01:29:36.950 --> 01:29:41.950
of moving forward, whereas
I see this particular era

01:29:42.670 --> 01:29:45.910
as very abrupt, very
different, very changing.

01:29:45.910 --> 01:29:50.270
But I'm reluctant to give
you a particular time.

01:29:50.270 --> 01:29:53.420
He also was interested in
the period of when Yale

01:29:53.420 --> 01:29:56.440
moved from being a seminary, if you will,

01:29:56.440 --> 01:30:00.030
that is training people
to go into the ministry,

01:30:00.030 --> 01:30:05.030
and it had its first
non-clerical president or rector.

01:30:07.822 --> 01:30:12.210
But I don't think he ever
has given me a specific date

01:30:12.210 --> 01:30:14.730
or time that he went along with.

01:30:14.730 --> 01:30:17.853
So I feel badly I can't
answer it, but I really can't.

01:30:19.270 --> 01:30:22.670
- So please join me in
thanking Sam Chauncey.

01:30:22.670 --> 01:30:25.070
That was fabulous.
- Thank you, Marta.

01:30:25.070 --> 01:30:30.070
- I mean really fabulous.
(audience applauding)

